{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again","id":1446441,"title":"So. Avatar: Legend of Korra politics came up once again","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-04-29T07:00:51-07:00","id":1446441,"title":"So. Avatar: Legend of Korra politics came up once again","content":"This is an offtopic from the Vampire:Masquerade. I think we can keep it here and see if it raises interest<br /> <br /> Also, want to promote this, it's pretty great. Critics say it's too indepth of an analisis for a childrens cartoon - but Korra explicitly wanted to be more than that. Korra wanted to touch adult themes and politics, so I think it's fair to do a serious analisis about it's political message:<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ModX151Ipgs&amp;ab_channel=KayAndSkittles\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ModX...KayAndSkittles</a><br /> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6alQz2CEsz0&amp;ab_channel=KayAndSkittles\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6alQ...KayAndSkittles</a><br /> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-DyKwTXPar4&amp;t=1s&amp;ab_channel=KayAndSkittles\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-DyK...KayAndSkittles</a><br /> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RGX2rRAlNME&amp;t=140s&amp;ab_channel=KayAndSkittles\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RGX2...KayAndSkittles</a> - here it's where actual Fascism comes in<br /> <br /> For the uninitiated, this started as a debate about Amon being Fascist or a Socialist<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Konradleijon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1446124-remember-when-they-said-there-is-gonna-be-a-tv-show?p=1446391#post1446391\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The reason why the metaphor doesn\u2019t work is because benders are not oppressed on the Avatar wolrd like Jews are in real life!<br /> <br /> Facists target easy scapegoats not popular sports players and religious figure.s</div> </div> </div> </div>You quoted the wrong post.<br /> <br /> But yeah, I agree, and then some<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> I feel like this is a rabbit hole that falls apart the same way that saying the Friends of Humanity and Sentinels have a point. Even if you can argue it, it's not something you can without destroying the entire basis of the franchise. When the protagonists are benders, I don't think you're meant to side with the people who want to exterminate them.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Friends of Humanity that I remember are mutant-phobes that attack mutants 'cuz they're diferent and scary, that thrive in a place where muntants clearly don't have all the social privileges<br /> .<br /> If mutants had social privileges on top of their powers, and humans were being sistematically abused by them, then FoH would have a point in spite of their despicable methods (humans won't just stop being harassed - you need to do something about that, too . Or you would say oppresion it's ok if the ones in power have cool mutant powers backing it?).<br /> <br /> One can debate how oppressed are non-benders in Korra book 1.<br /> <br /> On the side of systemic oppression:<br /> *It's shown that they suffer violence from benders<br /> *&quot;non benders&quot; aren't being represented by the political class (1rst non-bending president was stated to be a consequence of Amon's revolution).<br /> *A lot of jobs are only for benders, including all of the military and the police<br /> *There aren't official sports where non-benders can participate until book 2 (also made as a consequence of what Amon did in book 1)<br /> <br /> On the side of this oppresion being a sham:<br /> *Some non benders are rich?<br /> <br /> One can say that benders are privileged because they *are* better (they have powers), and thus it's not unjust at all.<br /> <br /> But then, defenders of real life status quo say, and trully believe, the same about poor people (elitism), and also about afro-americans (racism). The fact that a few members of a minority can ger rich it's also used as a way to show there isn't any oppresion by those that would defend the status quo.<br /> <br /> More so: &quot;a group of egalitarians that want to take away what makes you special, and balance everyone to the lowest common denominator&quot;<i> it's exactly what the far-rigth says about Socialism</i>.<br /> <br /> That commies are going to turn society into a bland mass of gray, poor/powerless, ppl has been the anty-communist preach since forever (&quot;distribution of poverty&quot;, &quot;they're going to take away your stuff and send you to live in a monoblock&quot;), and saying that Fascism it's the inevitable end result of Socialism (and the *real end goal* of all socialist leaders - obligatory Venezuela insert?) it's also very common among the rigth.<br /> <br /> Amon fails at being a Socialist because this facist distort of what true egalitarians want it's wrong, but the very fact that a muggle president was elected and a new sport was invented (<i>as if Amon had a point</i>) points towards the idea that the intention was to depict what they perceive as a <i>genuine problem of egalitarianism</i> , and not just an excuse for Facism.<br /> <br /> Whether intentionally or not, Amon fits right into what the far-right says about the dangers of Socialism - right into the whole fascist outbreak at the end of season 1. If the intention was for Amon to be a Nazi, like the dudes with the centinels in X-Men are, Korra did a bad job (if it needs to be said: Nazis haven't a point, much less *that* point) - Even ignoring the fact the actual ideology was never contested 'cuz Amon was a fraud that just wanted revenge all along.<br /> <br /> Also, Korra has book 4 for a dictatorship, complete with inner threats, segregation and concentration camps, I hear - legit Facists. And that does an even worse job than book 1 take on egalitarianism ... If <i>Kay And Skittles</i> it's rigth (I left in book 3 <i>tremendously </i>bad depiction of anarchism, so I leave that to the judgement of the ppl)<br /> <br /> I honestly think Korra's book 1 was the best - minus the ending. Loved Amon, loved the fear he inspired, loved the non-bender badass normal ninjas, hated that he was a fraud and the situation of non-benders was never touched upon again. It was a downhil experience from there","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 04-29-2021, 10:16 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T08:48:07-07:00","id":1446444,"title":"","content":"Yeah like their some rich black people. That doesn\u2019t mean racism doesn\u2019t exist. <br /> <br /> Imagine X-men if half of world leaders and politicians where mutants their where popular sports that where mutant only and everyone loved and jobs only mutants could do.<br /> Thank you for articulating my point! On how Korras equalists don\u2019t make sense as either a facist or communist allegory.<br /> <br /> Star Vs the Forces of Evil another kids show has a oppression metaphor and a racist and genocidal villan. But guess what they actually show Monsters being oppressed! Before the Xenophobic Not!Sailor moon shows up","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23952,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23952-konradleijon","user":"Konradleijon","date":". 04-29-2021, 12:06 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23952-konradleijon","name":"Konradleijon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23952_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1441"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T10:52:33-07:00","id":1446458,"title":"","content":"Amon was a pure bigot posing as whatever you think the leaders of the French Revolution were. He had a personal-<i>maybe</i>-turned-ideological grudge against the very existence of bending and gathered support for a campaign to wipe it out by appealing to nonbenders\u2019 sense of being second-class citizens and twisting it into genocidal sentiment against their perceived oppressors.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13313-sith_happens","name":"Sith_Happens","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13313_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T11:16:18-07:00","id":1446465,"title":"","content":"Yeah but Non-benders are second class citizens.<br /> <br /> Plus it\u2019s stupid to say bigotry comes from one pissed off guy with a personal grudge.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23952-konradleijon","name":"Konradleijon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23952_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1441"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T11:34:56-07:00","id":1446468,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sith_Happens</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1446458#post1446458\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Amon was a pure bigot posing as whatever you think the leaders of the French Revolution were. He had a personal-<i>maybe</i>-turned-ideological grudge against the very existence of bending and gathered support for a campaign to wipe it out by appealing to nonbenders\u2019 sense of being second-class citizens and twisting it into genocidal sentiment against their perceived oppressors.</div> </div> </div> </div>He was a traumatized child that had a thing against benders in general and the avatar in particular, and made a whole political movement to channel that.<br /> <br /> BUT <i>Toph in book 4 explicitly states that Amon really wanted equality, but went too far...</i><br /> <br /> ...which it's bullshit. Toph became more blind with her age, it seems. Yet you can't tell me wise old Toph wasn't the voice of the authors there, in that scene. Maybe the authors just forgot?<br /> <br /> But, you see, the thing is:<br /> <br /> The Equalists, regardless of what Amon wanted, had a <i>supposedly</i> egalitarian agenda. We never see how true it's that nonbenders are second-class citizens, we only have their word for it, and some vague hints (until the waterbender takes power, cuts the ligth of nonbender neigborhoods, and cordons them there until they rat the Equalists out. Then nonbenders are clearly depicted as second-class, whose livehoods can be denied in a whim)<br /> <br /> The egalitarian discourse against oppresion it's there (if not in Amon, in the nonbenders that folowed). And since that discourse it's never, ever, contested (ultimately Korra doesn't even try to prove that nonbenders aren't really being oppressed, she just &quot;unmasks&quot; the leader as a liar),we have little choice but grant it could be true (and the show does that too, when they elect the nonbender president).<br /> <br /> That Amon was just a fraud and a biggot all along, and never cared about equality, it's exactly what anty-communist say about any socialist leader ever: &quot;See how they live a rich life, while the poor toil. They say they're against unequality, but just want the riches for themselves&quot; - that Amon looses when he's discovered as part of the &quot;class of the oppressors&quot; it's very alegorical of that worldview<br /> .<br /> Clearly the authors have a wrong vision of what Socialism is or stands for. That vision fails to portray real life Socialism - in my humble opinion - BUT if you see what Capitalist Realism stands for, you will see that the vision of Socialism such worldview has, it's exactly what the Equalists (and even Amon) do in the show - complete with &quot;going the too far&quot; thing","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 04-29-2021, 02:53 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T12:05:20-07:00","id":1446472,"title":"","content":"Yeah Korra could not handle complex political themes at all. Maybe if they had simpler villains like Gangs? Wouldn\u2019t gangs make sense in the new setting of republic city?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23952-konradleijon","name":"Konradleijon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23952_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1441"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T13:08:11-07:00","id":1446483,"title":"","content":"I think part of the problem is that people are taking Amon at his word when Amon's motivations are due to the fact his father was an insane wizard. The leaders of Republic City are bender but it's not a requirement that they be Benders because we KNOW it's not.<br /> <br /> <img src=\"https://i.pinimg.com/originals/47/a5/20/47a520c63a6abbe4107313a104b2a881.jpg\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/><br /> <br /> Old Sokka was on the Council.<br /> <br /> The problems of Republic City are problems of CAPITALISM and the fact that the city's system are not providing enough jobs or a social safety net for the public. However, Amon is providing a scapegoat for them to hate. The fact they also have a pro-sport that only they can do is also a pretty weird line to draw for intolerables. Plenty of sports require bodies that other people don't ona professional level.<br /> <br /> The question is really, can ANYONE describe other than &quot;Benders can do stuff that other people can't&quot;, how lobotomizing or killing them helps the poverty and misery that lead to the Equalists?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That Amon was just a fraud and a bigot all along, and never cared about equality, it's exactly what anty-communist say about any socialist leader ever: &quot;See how they live a rich life, while the poor toil. They say they're against unequality, but just want the riches for themselves&quot; - that Amon looses when he's discovered as part of the &quot;class of the oppressors&quot; it's very alegorical of that worldview<br /> .<br /> Clearly the authors have a wrong vision of what Socialism is or stands for. That vision fails to portray real life Socialism - in my humble opinion - BUT if you see what Capitalist Realism stands for, you will see that the vision of Socialism such worldview has, it's exactly what the Equalists (and even Amon) do in the show - complete with &quot;going the too far&quot; thing </div> </div> </div>I feel like basically you're clinging very strongly to Amon, a man who is engaged in fraud WE KNOW TO BE TRUE (because he's a blood bender with a faked backstory), when we have an actually SINCERE leveler in Zaheer. The narrative also makes it clear that Zaheer is a better person than Amon too even if he's also got his own issues.<br /> <br /> So why defend Amon and not Zaheer?<br /> <br /> Why stick up for the guy who lies to his followers, practices minority based terrorism, preaches a system of fantastic racism versus Zaheer's economics based one, and generally is far more close to a fascist ideal?<br /> <br /> And while socialists are the opposite of fascists, there have been authoritarian socialists who were murderous pure evil. Because authoritarianism is always evil.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":12002,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","user":"CTPhipps","date":". 04-29-2021, 04:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T13:33:02-07:00","id":1446488,"title":"","content":"While I haven't had time to watch all the videos, or catch completely up on the discussion in the other thread:<br /> <br /> I don't feel it's fair to conflate socialism and communism as one thing; esp. the modern entirely non-Marxist Communism spread by the Stalin era and post-Stalin USSR. Are we actually going to argue that guys like Stalin, Mao, and Kim did communism correctly by Marxist principles? That they didn't provide capitalists the perfect anti-communist propaganda by being power hording corrupt asshats that killed millions instead of that being pure fiction?<br /> <br /> &quot;That Amon was just a fraud and a biggot all along, and never cared about equality, it's exactly what anty-communist say about any socialist leader ever.&quot; is a non-sense claim. It's what a dozen or so specific figures are framed as because... a lot of them were. The propaganda isn't that calling some really prominent bad socialists/communists out for being hypocrites, but never engaging with the history and legacy of all the Marxist/socialist/communist leaders that aren't those guys; and the hypocrisy of capitalist leaders of course. What the show gets wrong with the Equalists in historical context, is that the people that Amon represents don't start revolutionary movements: they supplant them with a totalitarian regime. You don't get Stalin without Lenin. You don't get Mao without Chen Duxiu,<br /> <br /> LoK either needed Amon to not be a fraud and Tarloc to be the Stalin/etc. figure that subverts his brother's movement, or needed an Equalist that was true to the ideals of the movement for Amon to supplant. A Lenin character to Amon-Stalin would have given the narrative space to explain the Equalist movement's goals and grievances without the taint of Amon's personal villainy.<br /> <br /> I also think it's not really a great critique to trying to have it both ways with some of the vague spots in the LoK story. &quot;This part of things in the story isn't fully explored or defined in the setting, so we have to assume the worst interpretation that just so happens to back up our critique's conclusion,&quot; isn't really that compelling of an argument.<br /> <br /> Edit:<br /> <br /> I also think there's a huge difference between &quot;not getting political ideologies,&quot; and not using them in the way a critic thinks is good.<br /> <br /> I don't think the LoK authors were actually trying to pen a take down of left-wing politics, or when left-wing politics goes &quot;too far.&quot; It seems far more likely that their intent was to use the political upheavals of the early 1900s are a macro-reflection of Korra's emotional journey. She is a literal embodiment of a traditional system that has met the end of its utility. Her struggle to figure out what being the Avatar means in a world that no longer wants to be subject to the whims of a sole unaccountable agent of balance wandering around doling out their personal view of justice, is paralleled by a society that doesn't know where its going and is far more defined by what it's trying to no longer be.<br /> <br /> This isn't to say that means the political messaging doesn't deserve criticism for being handled poorly, but if you're going to make claims about what the authors were trying to do, you're putting their intentions on the table.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":617,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","user":"Heavy Arms","date":". 04-29-2021, 04:43 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T14:06:51-07:00","id":1446492,"title":"","content":"Phipps Sokka was a respected war hero and friends with the Avatar/magic elemental Buddha. He isn\u2019t just a normal non bender. In 1870 Mississippi their was a black politician in Mississippi those that mean racism didn\u2019t exist in 1870s Mississippi?<br /> <br /> This has the theriom argument the writers chose to make Amon a lying hypocrites and to frame the equalists moment like nazis. Despite benders not being oppressed.<br /> <br /> Thinking about it further my biggest issue with the &quot;Equalists are supposed to be fascists&quot; is with whom they're opposing. Benders clearly have an advantage over non-Benders. We see Bender gangs extorting mundane shop-owners, Benders comprise the entirety of the government (that no one elects the Council is true but of little relevance), and in-general Benders objectively have advantages over non-Benders. Fascists... didn't oppose any group that fits those descriptions. The Jews weren't actually in power in Germany, and obviously, it's not any more applicable to socialists.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23952,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23952-konradleijon","user":"Konradleijon","date":". 04-29-2021, 05:15 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23952-konradleijon","name":"Konradleijon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23952_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1441"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T14:38:43-07:00","id":1446493,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Konradleijon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1446492#post1446492\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Phipps Sokka was a respected war hero and friends with the Avatar/magic elemental Buddha. He isn\u2019t just a normal non bender. In 1870 Mississippi their was a black politician in Mississippi those that mean racism didn\u2019t exist in 1870s Mississippi?<br /> <br /> This has the theriom argument the writers chose to make Amon a lying hypocrites and to frame the equalists moment like nazis. Despite benders not being oppressed.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, I do believe that the point of a show about Benders and the heroic nature of their power as a way to achieve social justice against unjust systems (Fire Nation, Equalists, Tyrants) is going to state that blaming Benders for social injustice is meant to be prejudice.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Thinking about it further my biggest issue with the &quot;Equalists are supposed to be fascists&quot; is with whom they're opposing. Benders clearly have an advantage over non-Benders. We see Bender gangs extorting mundane shop-owners, Benders comprise the entirety of the government (that no one elects the Council is true but of little relevance), and in-general Benders objectively have advantages over non-Benders. Fascists... didn't oppose any group that fits those descriptions. The Jews weren't actually in power in Germany, and obviously, it's not any more applicable to socialists. </div> </div> </div>1. Benders have superpowers but these powers do not provide them with financial success or guarantees of good lives. The biracial children of Republic City like Bolin and Mako are homeless street kids despite having the power to bend lightning and the Earth. Their power means NOTHING in terms of privilege socially or objectively.<br /> <br /> 2. This seems very much like an argument that fascists themselves would make. &quot;All Benders are bad because they do crimes.&quot; Replace that with Jews, Italians, or Blacks.<br /> <br /> 3. And we know that it is not a requirement that you have to be a Bender to be on the Council. Essentially, this is another fascist talking point. &quot;There's Jews in the government. Politicians meet with Jews! The Jews are controlling the government!&quot;<br /> <br /> 4. It basically goes further and ignores the immense amount of privilege the Equalists have due to the power they wield. They are supported by the equivalent of Henry Ford, a man who was a noted famous Anti-Semite himself, and wield vast amounts of power due to their rising tide of ethno-nationalism. Like the Pre-Coup Nazis, their paramilitary army gives them power in a supposedly just city.<br /> <br /> 5. Except, <b>Benders eventually DO end up Oppressed</b>. Ammon takes over Republic city and institutes a lobotomy of all Benders in the city. Even if I were to accept your argument, are they fascists then?<br /> <br /> Essentially, I think you're taking the Equalists as arguing in Good Faith and believing that because Anti-Bendism is a new phenomenon and not a longstanding prejudice that it can't be fascist. You've taken the view also that fascism=Punching down and communism=punching up. Except, the victims of Amon aren't just privileged Benders but the huge number of working class and even impoverished benders that exist.<br /> <br /> Because it's a racial allegory. People are born Benders or they aren't as Sokka's master shows.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":12002,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","user":"CTPhipps","date":". 04-29-2021, 05:42 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T14:54:02-07:00","id":1446496,"title":"","content":"As I mentioned before, another good video on the subject from Hello Future ME<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YcwExAXPkJk\" target=\"_blank\">Here</a>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/490-omenseer","name":"omenseer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar490_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"987"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T15:05:21-07:00","id":1446501,"title":"","content":"You do know white people can be poor and that doesn\u2019t invalidate racism? \u201cWhite trash\u201d doesn\u2019t mean white supremacy doesn\u2019t exist <br /> Also The Rothschilds doesn\u2019t mind antisemtism isn\u2019t real.<br /> <br /> Phipps are you white?<br /> <br /> anyone also have issues with how season 2 of Korra tried to tackle religious fundamentalism and theocracy with a indigenous coded animist!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23952,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23952-konradleijon","user":"Konradleijon","date":". 04-29-2021, 06:20 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23952-konradleijon","name":"Konradleijon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23952_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1441"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T15:41:34-07:00","id":1446506,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>omenseer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1446496#post1446496\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As I mentioned before, another good video on the subject from Hello Future ME<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YcwExAXPkJk\" target=\"_blank\">Here</a></div> </div> </div> </div>I've got to say, I do find this far more compelling. Trying to figure out what the authors are trying to say before looking at how well the allegories in a story works is, generally, a better literary analysis that something that feels like jumping the gun on what the allegories of LoK are, and then back-filling in why the strawman is bad.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T16:01:56-07:00","id":1446508,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1446493#post1446493\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1. Benders have superpowers but these powers do not provide them with financial success or guarantees of good lives. The biracial children of Republic City like Bolin and Mako are homeless street kids despite having the power to bend lightning and the Earth. Their power means NOTHING in terms of privilege socially or objectively.</div> </div> </div> </div>This.... really isn't how privilege works. Yes, some Benders don't benefit from privilege. Not all white people benefit from being white either. Benders dominate political life in the setting. Remember the Republic City is the most progressive part of the world in the setting. The elemental nations are all still ruled by Benders; even the relatively-compared-to-the-others Air and Metal Benders. Republic City is the only place non-Benders aren't inherently second class citizens, and even then not having superpowers puts them at a serious disadvantage.<br /> <br /> Mako is a homeless street kid somewhat by choice. He can do something lots of people can't: go to work making electricity for the city. He clearly views it as something like trying to make ends meet by getting paid to donate blood instead of work, while non-Benders don't have a whole set of jobs waiting for them that only non-Benders can do. Though he does humble up later.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 4. It basically goes further and ignores the immense amount of privilege the Equalists have due to the power they wield. </div> </div> </div>That's... not privilege. That's political power, yes, but those aren't the same thing. The Equalists (remembering that the vast majority of them are portrayed as normal citizens with at least some level of justified grievance at the inequality in their society) have to work hard for that power.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Even if I were to accept your argument, are they fascists then? </div> </div> </div>Not just to CT here...<br /> <br /> Can we stop slapping labels on everything? I think this conversation would be a lot more productive if people used some common definitions of these terms and justified labels if they're going to get used. Doing some things fascists do doesn't make you a fascist. Stalin was staunchly and honestly (by all historical accounts) anti-fascist despite his version of communism doing plenty of the same moral evils as Mussolini's government did.<br /> <br /> I don't see a case for the Equalists being fascists. Amon isn't even a fascist despite his deceptions. Fascism has some very specific aspects that are simply not present (such as high levels of nationalism) in the Equalist movement. That doesn't make them right, it just means the wrong words are being used to describe them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Because it's a racial allegory. People are born Benders or they aren't as Sokka's master shows. </div> </div> </div>No, it's an allegory about what makes up identities. Race is a fictional social narrative, Bending is a real difference. Usian Bolt and I construct our identities very differently. He has to incorporate being naturally advantaged in sprinting, I have to deal with having a very poor body for athletics. It's not about the color of our skin (though that does of course have impact because we exist in a world were the racial narrative is something we have to cope with).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T16:17:34-07:00","id":1446510,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Konradleijon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1446501#post1446501\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You do know white people can be poor and that doesn\u2019t invalidate racism? \u201cWhite trash\u201d doesn\u2019t mean white supremacy doesn\u2019t exist<br /> Also The Rothschilds doesn\u2019t mind antisemtism isn\u2019t real.<br /> <br /> Phipps are you white?</div> </div> </div> </div>I believe white privilege exists very strongly in terms of how people are treated by authority, institutions, and systemic privilege. I also believe that this is definitely why Benders are persecuted for their religious and ethnic minority. Mind you, I know that &quot;white privilege doesn't exist&quot; is a lie. It's nto a sincere belief and you claim it, you're disguising much more overt white supremacy. I grew up in Kentucky and the simple fact is that people who claim these things are not actually ignorant.<br /> <br /> No, they're malicious and the sooner you call them out as white supremacists the better. It's a liberal ignorance that their enemies aren't racists and bigots.<br /> <br /> If you say white privilege doesn't exist, you are a white supremacist. That is my belief.<br /> <br /> So does this mean I am a Bender supremacist?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> anyone also have issues with how season 2 of Korra tried to tackle religious fundamentalism and theocracy with a indigenous coded animist! </div> </div> </div>Reactionary religious fundamentalists led by a man trying to be the Anti-Christ is why Season 2 is the worst Season.<br /> <br /> But seriously, I worry about people trying to &quot;both sides&quot; a group that proposes genocide.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't see a case for the Equalists being fascists. Amon isn't even a fascist despite his deceptions. Fascism has some very specific aspects that are simply not present (such as high levels of nationalism) in the Equalist movement. That doesn't make them right, it just means the wrong words are being used to describe them.</div> </div> </div> </div>He's proposing a militaristic ethno-state that scapegoats minorities and uses an appeal to Tradition (The Spirits) to justify it. It fits most of Umberto Eco's 14 points.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://www.openculture.com/2016/11/umberto-eco-makes-a-list-of-the-14-common-features-of-fascism.html\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.openculture.com/2016/11/...f-fascism.html</a><br /> <br /> I think if you fall into the trap that, &quot;Mutants are the real bigots!&quot; I hate in X-men fandom.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":12002,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","user":"CTPhipps","date":". 04-29-2021, 07:21 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T16:22:55-07:00","id":1446513,"title":"","content":"<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">This isn't to say that means the political messaging doesn't deserve criticism for being handled poorly, but if you're going to make claims about what the authors were trying to do, you're putting their intentions on the table.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm just focusing on the political message . I do think that the authors were <i>intending </i>to criticize the dangers of Communism - And I do, and likely will, conflate Communism with Socialism for entirely offtopic reasons. But I'm willing not to do that in this thread, if you want. (if you're curious about the reasons: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KEC2ajsvr0I&amp;ab_channel=Hakim\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KEC2..._channel=Hakim</a>)<br /> <br /> In that case let me reframe: It's a debate about Amon being a Fascist or a Communist<br /> <br /> I don't know if the authors <i>intended </i>to &quot;to pen a take down of left-wing politics, or when left-wing politics goes &quot;too far&quot;&quot; - but if they didn't intended to do that, the fact that they did shows how pervasive it's this kind of tought.<br /> <br /> That it's what Capitalist Realism it's all about - it's not an ideology, and thus has nothing to do with <i>intent</i> - Capitalist Realism it's the fact that people can't seem to see any option to the status quo that doesn't end in disaster. As Mark Fisher, said: &quot;It's easier to see the end of the world than the end of Capitalism&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That Amon was just a fraud and a biggot all along, and never cared about equality, it's exactly what anty-communist say about any socialist leader ever.&quot; is a non-sense claim. It's what a dozen or so specific figures are framed as because... a lot of them were. </div> </div> </div>Ok. My point was that it fits within a discourse, I never intended to analize if said discourse was rigth or wrong about pointing that out. That Amon fits within that Communist, let's say, &quot;model&quot; better than with a Facist one ... was the whole point here.<br /> <br /> Once we've determined that Korra book 1 it's, indeed, about Communism, it makes sense to start pondering if the representation of Communism was good or bad. You seem to conclude it was bad:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The propaganda isn't that calling some really prominent bad socialists/communists out for being hypocrites, but never engaging with the history and legacy of all the Marxist/socialist/communist leaders that aren't those guys; and the hypocrisy of capitalist leaders of course. What the show gets wrong with the Equalists in historical context, is that the people that Amon represents don't start revolutionary movements: they supplant them with a totalitarian regime. You don't get Stalin without Lenin. You don't get Mao without Chen Duxiu, </div> </div> </div>And I agree.<br /> <br /> But back to the origin: Note that Korra made his own dictator - so, would you say it was trying to talk about the problems of Communism? (and did a poor job), of Facism?, of neither...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 04-29-2021, 07:34 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T16:29:06-07:00","id":1446514,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Konradleijon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1446501#post1446501\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">anyone also have issues with how season 2 of Korra tried to tackle religious fundamentalism and theocracy with a indigenous coded animist!</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, my problems with Korra only increase overtime<br /> <br /> Good thing I didn't saw part 4, because as someone from a country that suffered from an actual dictatoship, that claimed the extermination of political opponents to be a consequence of the figth against guerrillas, I think I would have gotten quite mad to saw such a misrepresentation there<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T16:36:35-07:00","id":1446519,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1446483#post1446483\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I feel like basically you're clinging very strongly to Amon, a man who is engaged in fraud WE KNOW TO BE TRUE (because he's a blood bender with a faked backstory)</div> </div> </div> </div>I hate when I have to do this:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Equalists,<b> regardless of what Amon wanted</b>, had a <i>supposedly </i>egalitarian agenda. We never see how true it's that nonbenders are second-class citizens, we only have their word for it, and some vague hints (until the waterbender takes power, cuts the ligth of nonbender neigborhoods, and cordons them there until they rat the Equalists out. Then nonbenders are clearly depicted as second-class, whose livehoods can be denied in a whim)<br /> <br /> <b>The egalitarian discourse against oppresion it's there </b>(if not in Amon, in the nonbenders that folowed). And since<b> that discourse it's never, ever, contested</b> (ultimately Korra doesn't even try to prove that nonbenders aren't really being oppressed, <b>she just &quot;unmasks&quot; the leader as a liar</b>),we have little choice but grant it could be true (and the show does that too, when they elect the nonbender president).</div> </div> </div> </div>Amon being a fraud it's an easy scapegoat to refute the whole gig, and thus part of the problem of representation. You can't analize anything by clinging on the guy, because he never believed in his own politics (unless Toph was rigth, then I don't know what to say). I try to AVOID clinging on Amon, and rather focus on the politics he used as an excuse.<br /> <br /> Not saying the Equalists were rigth either, mind you, just saying they were a poor mis-representation of Communism and not a clever commentary on Facism<br /> <br /> Not saying this was intentional either.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> when we have an actually SINCERE leveler in Zaheer. The narrative also makes it clear that Zaheer is a better person than Amon too even if he's also got his own issues.<br /> <br /> So why defend Amon and not Zaheer?<br /> <br /> Why stick up for the guy who lies to his followers, practices minority based terrorism, preaches a system of fantastic racism versus Zaheer's economics based one, and generally is far more close to a fascist ideal?</div> </div> </div> </div>Why does it look like I'm defending Amon?. I'm just saying Korra had a bogus take on politics, obviously Amon and the Equalists being completely ludicrous in their approach to Communism, it's an integral part of it<br /> <br /> I'm not going to defend Zaheer because he's to anarchism what Amon it's to comunism. Zaheer never really worked with the people, like anarchists do - he just killed the queen, and then the city spontaneously catched fire.<br /> <br /> Because, of course, the city catching fire it's what happens when anarchists take the power. Right???? Anarchism making cities catch fire it's as much a meme of Anarchism as &quot;Distribution of Poverty&quot; it's a meme about Communism<br /> <br /> See the third video. It's about Anarchism in Korra.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Old Sokka was on the Council. </div> </div> </div>Well, you can take that one down from the list.<br /> <br /> Albeit, then again, Sokka was a big hero and friend of the Avatar...I'm not sure he makes a really good example for real life cases where lack of representation it's being debated IRL (glass ceiling and all). He's certainly not mentioned in that context, he's just shown as part of a flashback - Heck, we don't even know if the legislation changed.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The problems of Republic City are problems of CAPITALISM and the fact that the city's system are not providing enough jobs or a social safety net for the public.</div> </div> </div> </div>Are they now? , the hobo in shanty town seemed very happy, wellcomed Korra and talks about how equal they are down there<br /> <br /> I think that what's going on it's a romantization of those problems.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> However, Amon is providing a scapegoat for them to hate. The fact they also have a pro-sport that only they can do is also a pretty weird line to draw for intolerables. Plenty of sports require bodies that other people don't ona professional level. </div> </div> </div>Nonbenders can do sports, they just don't seem to have official sports backed by a lot of money, and for some reason that only changed after Amon. I take it as a &quot;vague hint&quot; of oppression, not as proof, tough. it's just weird<br /> <br /> Sports and discrimination have a long story. Women had, and still have, to fight for their sports to get recognition. I could see that as ppl thinking &quot;nobody would watch nonbenders doing sports&quot;, but that's just a speculation<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The question is really, can ANYONE describe other than &quot;Benders can do stuff that other people can't&quot;, how lobotomizing or killing them helps the poverty and misery that lead to the Equalists? </div> </div> </div>Amon describes the problem as benders starting wars, and generally enacting violence and ruling by force. But don't miss the forest for the trees: I never said Amon's take, nor his solution, made any sense.<br /> <br /> My point it's that the whole representation it's a lot similar as how anti-communists depict Communism. That doesn't need to mean the brothers are anti-communists, that's a very present opinion in ordinary common sense<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> We never see how true it's that nonbenders are second-class citizens, we only have their word for it, and some vague hints</div> </div> </div> </div>If I'm rigth, and Equalists are a poor representation of Communism, that the oppresion they call out it's never clearly depicted it's part of the problems of such poor representation. As the video says, the first nonbending oppresion we see it's when Amon's brother takes the power. In the end, one could say nonbenders were exaggerating and their problems with Capitalism weren't so dire.<br /> <br /> After all, the hobo of shanty town it's always similing, what a nice dude.<br /> <br /> We never see these problems with Capitalism being explored outside of &quot;Benders can do stuff that other people can't&quot;, and that it's part of the whole misconstruing. Not to sound like a broken reccord, but Communists taking advantage of &quot;poor being jealous of the rich&quot; it's classic anty-Communism","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 04-29-2021, 07:38 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T16:38:45-07:00","id":1446520,"title":"","content":"Well my only statement on the &quot;Equalists=Communists&quot; is it's very weird to have the Robber Baron support the communist analog.<br /> <br /> Benders=The Rich<br /> <br /> Just does not work as an analog.<br /> <br /> Especially when the richest man in the setting is a Muggle.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T17:18:05-07:00","id":1446526,"title":"","content":"Huh, usually my multi-posts work...:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1446510#post1446510\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I also believe that this is definitely why Benders are persecuted for their religious and ethnic minority.</div> </div> </div> </div>Besides a very short lived Equalist control of Republic City.... where is this true in the setting? Benders control ever other location in the world, and still end up with serious power in Republic City after the Equalist movement disperses.<br /> <br /> Benders are also not a distinct ethnicity, or religion. Bending is a physical ability that impacts the cultures of the setting by virtue of existing. But there isn't a separate Air Bender ethnicity or religion from the Air Nomads that don't Bend.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> He's proposing a militaristic ethno-state that scapegoats minorities and uses an appeal to Tradition (The Spirits) to justify it. It fits most of Umberto Eco's 14 points. </div> </div> </div>I think you might be conflating antagonists here. Amon doesn't propose a militaristic ethno-state, he doesn't scapegoat a political minority, and uses an appeal to change (getting rid of Benders) not tradition (having Benders in-charge, and Bending comes from spirits). The closest to an appeal to tradition is that Bending isn't actually natural... is something humans gained from spirits... but it's questionable if Amon actually knew that compared to just feeling that way from his upbringing.<br /> <br /> I'd also love to see you're counting of those 14 points because I don't get &quot;most&quot; when I try. And &quot;most&quot; isn't really enough to me, esp. since Eco is describing proto-fascism, not actual fascism. &quot;These are the signs you're at risk of getting fascists,&quot; isn't the same as, &quot;getting X out of 14 pints means you're a fascist.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1446513#post1446513\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm just focusing on the political message .</div> </div> </div> </div>Then why are you trying to justify stating what the creator's opinions about socialism are for them?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I do think that the authors were <i>intending </i>to criticize the dangers of Communism </div> </div> </div>I see no case to this. It doesn't fit what the authors have said. It doesn't actually fit the story itself.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> (if you're curious about the reasons: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KEC2ajsvr0I&amp;ab_channel=Hakim\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KEC2..._channel=Hakim</a>) </div> </div> </div>I'd prefer you use your own words. I have no interest in a video rant with a title that denotes such a strong bad faith position, and I'd rather assume you're not advocating such either.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It's a debate about Amon being a Fascist or a Communist </div> </div> </div>Neither, obviously.<br /> <br /> The big problem? Amon doesn't have a governing philosophy. He cares about taking power from Benders (something his brother says is an honest belief well before Toph did) because he feels the power that comes from Bending is inherently corrupting. He never states any ideology about how to organize society, manage economics, or deal with foreign relations.<br /> <br /> Amon is a revolutionary that wants to overturn the current status quo, but expresses zero inclination towards what to do with society after the Benders are removed (presumably because he either doesn't think he'll survive to Benders completely eliminated in his lifetime, or is perhaps actually fanatical enough to kill himself if somehow Bending was destroyed).<br /> <br /> Even the wider Equalist movement doesn't have a fully formed political ideology. It has a very distinct political goal, but no clear unity on anything beyond that goal.<br /> <br /> It's a complete false dichotomy to say that Amon has to be one or the other. The evidence for either is so lacking I'm baffled as to why anyone would want to center the conversation around it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't know if the authors <i>intended </i>to &quot;to pen a take down of left-wing politics, or when left-wing politics goes &quot;too far&quot;&quot; - but if they didn't intended to do that, the fact that they did shows how pervasive it's this kind of tought. </div> </div> </div>Considering the total story arc of the show is that being the agent of the status quo is, in fact, the wrong choice, and that clear intended moral is that fearing change because of what might go wrong needs to be overcome to make the world a better place... maybe you and the authors are closer in actual opinions that you're prepared to accept simply because their show wasn't as well crafted as it could have been.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That it's what Capitalist Realism it's all about - it's not an ideology, and thus has nothing to do with <i>intent</i> - Capitalist Realism it's the fact that people can't seem to see any option to the status quo that doesn't end in disaster. As Mark Fisher, said: &quot;It's easier to see the end of the world than the end of Capitalism&quot; </div> </div> </div>While the authors do not craft an option other than the status quo, Korra accepts that her role is to stop being the agent of it, and become the agent of something else in the finale refutes this being something that applies to the politics of the show.<br /> <br /> The odd thing is that Aang's story is far more subject to Capitalist Realism. Aang's story is literally about returning things to the traditional balance of power that has dominated the world for centuries, despite all the clear moral wrongs that setup entails, because changing the world in a meaningful way doesn't even occur to him. The most progress Aang achieves in his whole life, which is covered in LoK, is getting the four great nations to establish one neutral city to encourage cultural exchange and understanding between them. It's less progressive than founding the UN.<br /> <br /> Korra's whole character arc is her getting over being highly subject to this kind of thinking. She can't see the Avatar as anything other that trying to be Aang, the great unifier and defender of balance. She can't imagine who she is without Bending. She literally has an easier time coping with the idea that she might die, than she does with dealing with meaningful personal and societal change. In the end? She completely upends the status quo of the setting. She might not know what the option that isn't the status quo is, but she literally goes out looking for it at the end of the story instead of falling back to the status quo.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Ok. My point was that it fits within a discourse, I never intended to analize if said discourse was rigth or wrong about pointing that out. That Amon fits within that Communist, let's say, &quot;model&quot; better than with a Facist one ... was the whole point here. </div> </div> </div>This feels more like goal post shift to me? But if that's your point... I'm not sure what the point is? Amon can be slotted into anti-communist talking points. Great. He can be fit into anti-lots-of-things talking points. So... what?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Once we've determined that Korra book 1 it's, indeed, about Communism, it makes sense to start pondering if the representation of Communism was good or bad. You seem to conclude it was bad: </div> </div> </div>I conclude that Korra book 1 isn't about communism and claiming it is boggles my mind. It's a bad representation of communism because it isn't meant to be one, and doesn't actually become one even by accident.<br /> <br /> The X-Men aren't about fascism vs. communism either, though the X-Men and the Avatar series both struggle as allegories for the same reason (people are born with superpowers only goes so far as metaphor for how power disparities play out in reality).<br /> <br /> So, again, neither is the clear and obvious choice. The Communism vs. Fascism debate is something that, from my perspective, is being forced on the show by people that want to make points about how communism and fascism are misrepresented in a broader context rather than a well reasoned discussion about what's in the show itself. The idea that your options are communism or fascism as if that's all there is out there is a huge reason for my position.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":617,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","user":"Heavy Arms","date":". 04-29-2021, 08:46 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T17:34:08-07:00","id":1446531,"title":"","content":"What I am saying is that the Equalists are a terrible take on fascism and how bigotry works. First one person caused a extremist hate group to form. Which isn\u2019t how hatred works. People where super racist against Jews,Catholicos, and blacks before the KKK was founded. Anti sentiism wasn\u2019t extinguished when Hitler died. After the first season we see no Remnants of The equalists, and second the groups hate groups target are already marginalized under the current system! Black people still face systematic racism and antisemtism goes way back in Europe. Bender discrimination doesn\u2019t seem to have any roots it\u2019s what one perosn made.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23952-konradleijon","name":"Konradleijon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23952_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1441"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T17:41:31-07:00","id":1446534,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1446520#post1446520\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Especially when the richest man in the setting is a Muggle.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, *that guy* it's quite a specimen. He's the n1 proff of the setting being heavily influenced by Capitalist Realism. He makes wars for proffit, kidnapped the president, and made a nuke and tons of weapons for the literal Nazi. Yet he's represented in an extremely sympathetic ligth, and gets left off the hook quite easily<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Well my only statement on the &quot;Equalists=Communists&quot; is it's very weird to have the Robber Baron support the communist analog.<br /> <br /> Benders=The Rich<br /> <br /> Just does not work as an analog. </div> </div> </div>it trully doesn't. A metaphor, on the other hand, it's a very different thing.<br /> <br /> Since money actually does exist in the Avatar-verse, it could be better framed as metaphor of <i>social inequality.</i> Again, some muggles having money isn't really the same as muggles not being oppressed or disadvantaged.<br /> <br /> IRL, a women it's the head of the IMF. Would you say men aren't privileged, then?<br /> <br /> Ultimately it's wrong, because Amon it's wrong and the Equalists are wrong. of course they are, they were made to be wrong in the same way as the hobo from shanty town was made to be a nice romatized version of poverty...but that's how their discourse rolls","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 04-29-2021, 08:52 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T18:07:08-07:00","id":1446535,"title":"","content":"I do note its weird there don't seem to be that many rich Benders.<br /> <br /> 1. Varric<br /> 2. Saito.<br /> 3. Cabbage Guy<br /> <br /> is it because technology is anti-bending and the Steampunk revolution has made Bending obsolete like the gun did martial arts?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T19:04:58-07:00","id":1446541,"title":"","content":"Benders don't present a lot of riches, but vast amounts of wealth. The majority of Bender economic power is rooted in pre-Industrial land ownership. While Zuko was Lord of the Fire Nation, think about how much wealth he controlled. We see a lot of landed aristocracy among the Benders, and very few industrially affluent bourgeoisie Benders.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T20:11:43-07:00","id":1446548,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Then why are you trying to justify stating what the creator's opinions about socialism are for them?</div> </div> </div> </div>The political message of the work doesn't reflect the creator's opinions? True, maybe it doesn't.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'd prefer you use your own words. </div> </div> </div>Now THAT rabbit hole would require it's own thread. This one it's full with Amon, and I was hoping to rant about the other books too. Perhaps more so.<br /> <br /> So, sadly, I have to decline. Maybe I can make another thread to talk about socialism and communism, in another time.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Even the wider Equalist movement doesn't have a fully formed political ideology. It has a very distinct political goal, but no clear unity on anything beyond that goal.<br /> <br /> It's a complete false dichotomy to say that Amon has to be one or the other. The evidence for either is so lacking I'm baffled as to why anyone would want to center the conversation around it. </div> </div> </div>The aesthetics and discourse?, I'm willing to go with <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/490-omenseer\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar490_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"490\" data-vbnamecard=\"490\">omenseer</a> 's video about Equalists not being Communism, but a sui-generis form of Populism instead. Then again, that's one word that covers a lot of terrain, and it's more often than not just a derogatory<br /> <br /> The only villan from Korra that declares adhesion to a real life political movement it's Zaheer. So nobody would say he's not intended to be an anarchist, rigth?<br /> <br /> Problem is: Zaheer philosophy of chaos <i>has nothing to do with anarchism, either</i>. Or does it?. I mean: He expected the city to burn, and was happy with the results, because <i>disharmony it's harmony</i>. I wasn't aware anarchists believed in anything like that, much less that burning the city was more than just an anti-anarchist meme.<br /> <br /> He has no real ideology about how to organize society (well, chaos it's organization, I guess...), manage economics, or deal with foreign relations either - he just has to kill the opressors, and then everything will fall into place because that's the natural law.<br /> <br /> Is Zaheer a (poorly made) criticism of the problems of Anarchism? If so, is there any other 100% certain reason to call him that, besides that he calls himself an anarchist?<br /> <br /> The problem I see, it's that a misconstrued ideology necessarily it's going to lack certain elements, and a lot can be glossed over.<br /> <br /> What convinces me more of Amon <b><i>not </i></b>representing a misconception about Communism, it's not what he <i>lacks </i>but that there are some aspects of his ideology that seem like weird additions. Like the whole thing with bending being &quot;impurities&quot; - maybe I'm wrong, but I've never seen someone state that any kind of socialists think privileges are &quot;impurities&quot;, even in the worst misconceptions about socialism. Such <i>additions</i> can't be attributes to a lack of development<br /> <br /> Then again, I see that a lot of this enters into &quot;conspiracy theory&quot; territory when we can't clearly pinpoint stuff. Arguing over different &quot;dogwhistles&quot; it's no different that having &quot;hunches&quot; about what the message is, I could concede that's not a correct way to go about this - at least, it's not something that one can really <i>agree </i>uppon<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Considering the total story arc of the show is that being the agent of the status quo is, in fact, the wrong choice, and that clear intended moral is that fearing change because of what might go wrong needs to be overcome to make the world a better place... maybe you and the authors are closer in actual opinions that you're prepared to accept simply because their show wasn't as well crafted as it could have been. </div> </div> </div>Oh, I'm inclined to believe so.<br /> <br /> From the very beggining - hence why I blamed a pervasive way of thinking, that's part of society, and very hard to deal with<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> While the authors do not craft an option other than the status quo, Korra accepts that her role is to stop being the agent of it, and become the agent of something else in the finale refutes this being something that applies to the politics of the show.<br /> <br /> She completely upends the status quo of the setting. She might not know what the option that isn't the status quo is, but she literally goes out looking for it at the end of the story instead of falling back to the status quo. </div> </div> </div>Can't comment, cuz I haven't seen the finale - but kudos for that.<br /> <br /> I really hated the reset-button that went after Amon. Or that opening the door to the frigging spirit worlds doesn't managed to disturb the status-quo as much as one would think (I mean, other than ppl now having to weed some bothersome plants we don't see much of the spirits afterwards - they were more present when the door was closed :P - well, it caused Zaheer to gain beinding too, admitedly)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The odd thing is that Aang's story is far more subject to Capitalist Realism. Aang's story is literally about returning things to the traditional balance of power that has dominated the world for centuries, despite all the clear moral wrongs that setup entails, because changing the world in a meaningful way doesn't even occur to him. The most progress Aang achieves in his whole life, which is covered in LoK, is getting the four great nations to establish one neutral city to encourage cultural exchange and understanding between them. It's less progressive than founding the UN. </div> </div> </div>Not all traditional values nor all kinds of status-quo can be refered as Capitalist Realism<br /> <br /> If there's a paralel in Legend of Aang, it's with colonization and imperialism. They're all natives figthing to retain their cultural heritage in the verge of the expansionist (and heavily industrialized - like England) Fire Kingdom.<br /> <br /> Nobody would say the native americans wanting to reject the change brougth by europeans it's Capitalist Realism, and certainly I can't see how that applies to Legend of Aang political arena<br /> <br /> There wasn't much Capitalism to upend in LoA world, in the first place - and that it's quite an important element in such a concept, as the name would indicate<br /> <br /> If anything, the world changes to become more capitalist by embracing cultural exchange in Capital City, and the process of industrialization that was started by the Fire Nation. Because this change it's obviously a good thing, it's the closest to such a concept as Capitalist Realism I can think off in LoA (as in, the world <i>obviously </i>has to evolve in that direction - but that's a very small thing).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This feels more like goal post shift to me? But if that's your point... I'm not sure what the point is? Amon can be slotted into anti-communist talking points. Great. He can be fit into anti-lots-of-things talking points. So... what? </div> </div> </div>If it can apply to anti-lots-of-things, then my point was just wrong.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 04-29-2021, 11:32 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T20:12:31-07:00","id":1446549,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1446541#post1446541\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Benders don't present a lot of riches, but vast amounts of wealth. The majority of Bender economic power is rooted in pre-Industrial land ownership. While Zuko was Lord of the Fire Nation, think about how much wealth he controlled. We see a lot of landed aristocracy among the Benders, and very few industrially affluent bourgeoisie Benders.</div> </div> </div> </div>Very true but nobility doesn't = Bender either, even if in this world you'd think it would.<br /> <br /> The Earth King and Queen isn't necessarily a Bender, for example. Indeed, they very often seem not to be.<br /> <br /> Part of what made the Fire Nation evil was that its royal family was obsessed with its members being Benders and involving itself in Eugenics.<br /> <br /> Its also not at all relevant to the Air Nomads because every Air Nomad was a bender and they practiced communal nomadism with no accumulation of wealth whatsoever but shared property.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Problem is: Zaheer philosophy of chaos <i>has nothing to do with anarchism, either</i>. Or does it?. I mean: He expected the city to burn, and was happy with the results, because <i>disharmony it's harmony</i>. I wasn't aware anarchists believed in anything like that, much less that burning the city was more than just an anti-anarchist meme.</div> </div> </div> </div>Speaking as an actual Anarchist, Zaheer's philosophy is actually completely accurate to certain schools of Anarchy that have the view that the people who overthrow oppression SHOULD NOT attempt to impose a new system themselves. It's literally what they believe divides them from revolutionaries and tyrants. The belief is that an anarchist's purpose is to cast down corrupt systems and then THE PEOPLE AS A WHOLE will decide on creating a fairer and more just system.<br /> <br /> So Zaheer having no plans to replace or take over after killing the Earth Queen is not a bug but what he assumes will lead to reform from the people banding together because people are naturally good.<br /> <br /> As we see with Kuvira, the Earth Bender version of Mao, it is a wrong assumption.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":12002,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","user":"CTPhipps","date":". 04-29-2021, 11:17 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T20:59:04-07:00","id":1446553,"title":"","content":"I don't think the Equalists are supposed to stand in for Communism, Fascism or Socialism. I think they take cues from some of those positions, but trying to figure out which one they're supposed to stand in for seems like putting square pegs into round holes.<br /> <br /> The Equalists are essentially a response to an issue that exists only within Avatar's world. If a large portion of society had superpowers, then simply possessing those powers puts them on an elevated position above those who don't. In a society that treasures the power of Bending to the degree that the Avatar world does, then the ability to Bend lends the individual in question a privileged position in society above that of the average non-Bender.<br /> It's worth noting that Bending doesn't seem to be the be-all-and-end-all of status in Avatar's world. The Earth King in the Last Airbender wasn't himself a Bender and neither was his daughter in the Legend of Korra. Still, a Bender always has something that a non-Bender lacks, something that society sees as extremely valuable.<br /> The Equalists are the response to this issue, not any of the social problems of -our- world.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Raksi plays Peek-a-boo for keeps. ~ nalak42","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/203-kleptomania","name":"Kleptomania","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar203_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"557"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T21:11:33-07:00","id":1446554,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kleptomania</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1446553#post1446553\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think the Equalists are supposed to stand in for Communism, Fascism or Socialism. I think they take cues from some of those positions, but trying to figure out which one they're supposed to stand in for seems like putting square pegs into round holes.<br /> <br /> The Equalists are essentially a response to an issue that exists only within Avatar's world. If a large portion of society had superpowers, then simply possessing those powers puts them on an elevated position above those who don't. In a society that treasures the power of Bending to the degree that the Avatar world does, then the ability to Bend lends the individual in question a privileged position in society above that of the average non-Bender.<br /> It's worth noting that Bending doesn't seem to be the be-all-and-end-all of status in Avatar's world. The Earth King in the Last Airbender wasn't himself a Bender and neither was his daughter in the Legend of Korra. Still, a Bender always has something that a non-Bender lacks, something that society sees as extremely valuable.<br /> The Equalists are the response to this issue, not any of the social problems of -our- world.</div> </div> </div> </div>There's also the irony that society is moving PAST Bending as a status marker in Avatar. The Fire Nation, ironically, the one most heavily reliant on air bending has created technology that renders it useless.<br /> <br /> So the resentment and hatred of Benders comes at the time when they're the most useless.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T22:13:46-07:00","id":1446559,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1446548#post1446548\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The political message of the work doesn't reflect the creator's opinions?</div> </div> </div> </div>There's a difference between how a work reflects on creators, and stating directly that you feel you know what the creator was intending based solely on inferences from the text; esp. with statements from the creators about their intentions.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The aesthetics and discourse? </div> </div> </div>We can have a discourse about the politics of LoK without making it an unjustified choice between two mutually exclusive options. So... I don't know what aesthetics or discourse in making this uncritically about communism vs. fascism.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Then again, that's one word that covers a lot of terrain, and it's more often than not just a derogatory </div> </div> </div>And... given the Western bias of creators of the show, the audience of the show, and the users of this board.... communist and fascist don't have derogatory connotations?<br /> <br /> Populism is a far more accurate term. Given how charged most of these terms are, accuracy feels more important to me.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The only villan from Korra that declares adhesion to a real life political movement it's Zaheer. So nobody would say he's not intended to be an anarchist, rigth?<br /> <br /> Problem is: Zaheer philosophy of chaos <i>has nothing to do with anarchism, either</i>. Or does it? </div> </div> </div>Zaheer isn't a collectivist anarchist like most left-anarchists are. Zaheer practices a form of highly individualistic anarcho-primitivism that is something argued for by some real world anarchist philosophers; as CT has noted. So, yes, Zaheer is an anarchist, even if he advocates a very specific strain of anarchism, and one that's frequently misrepresented (and that strawman is then used against anarchism in general) because of its closeness to chaotic anarchy and revolutionary tactics.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> (well, chaos it's organization, I guess...)...everything will fall into place because that's the natural law. </div> </div> </div>Yep. That's the stance of his strain of anarchism: the plan is no plan, but purposefully so rather than unthinkingly so.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Is Zaheer a (poorly made) criticism of the problems of Anarchism? </div> </div> </div>I don't see his role as a criticism of anarchism, though anarchism is lazily used as a shorthand for &quot;bad revolutionary&quot; for what I perceive as the point of his philosophy in the story.<br /> <br /> Keep in mind that, unlike Amon, Zaheer returns and does so to help Korra instead of fight her, as he sees Kuvira as his true antithesis.<br /> <br /> Zaheer's political point is to be a foil to Aang's legacy. Where Aang takes the Air Bender philosophy towards a technical pacifist and minimal interventionist position (despite being the Avatar who is both a combatant and intervener), Zaheer's role is to show how that can't generate change, and takes the Air Bender philosophy as a reason to use violence and intervention to free people from the fetters that weigh them down.<br /> <br /> Rather than a criticism of anarchism specifically, Zaheer is a criticism of &quot;The One&quot; tropes around Aang, because he's really doing exactly what Aang and the Gaang were doing, but for reasons that aren't presented as sympathetic to the audience. Zaheer is that to negate the audience's assumptions around the validity of One Great Man political theory that applies so heavily to how the world of Avatar is at default.<br /> <br /> While not fair to anarchism in the sense of explaining its theories in detail, anarchism is a natural political stance for a character that stands in opposition to One Great Man theory.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If so, is there any other 100% certain reason to call him that, besides that he calls himself an anarchist? </div> </div> </div>As noted above, his claim towards being within the anarchist umbrella is valid, which is more than enough reason to call him that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Can't comment, cuz I haven't seen the finale - but kudos for that. </div> </div> </div>It does seem strange to attempt to analyze the politics of a show without taking the entirety of the show into account. While nothing recontextualzies Amon in a significant fashion later, the point of the different antagonists and Korra's reactions to them is written as a more cohesive statement.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Not all traditional values nor all kinds of status-quo can be refered as Capitalist Realism </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure what the value of the concept is to this discussion if it can't be applied beyond the strict realms of a capitalist society. Capitalism, even by the time of LoK, is in its infancy in the setting. It's neither traditional or the status quo of the world in the story. If the authors have literally penned a world where capitalism has barely arisen and seems like it is at significant risk of not spreading... it doesn't seem like it can apply to the authors either.<br /> <br /> The concept of &quot;it's easier to picture the end of the world than picture something different from our default status quo,&quot; doesn't seem something uniquely capitalist to me, and removed to the level of a more natural human inclination, it seems justified to phrase Aang's reversion of thing to the status quo from before his imprisonment as this sort of thinking.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If there's a paralel in Legend of Aang, it's with colonization and imperialism. They're all natives figthing to retain their cultural heritage in the verge of the expansionist (and heavily industrialized - like England) Fire Kingdom. </div> </div> </div>While the Water Tribes are certainly coded with polar native identity, it feels exceeding off to paint the Fire Kingdom as having that level of power disparity; esp. with the Earth Kingdom around.<br /> <br /> First, the four Nations coexisted for an extremely long time before the removal of the Avatar unbalanced the situation.<br /> <br /> Second, the Fire Nation's strategy, while certainly expansionist, wasn't particularly colonialist. The Air Nomads were wiped out to delay the Avatar Cycle. The Southern Water Tribe was attacked precisely in the hopes of continuing that delay as the Water Tribes were the next in line for the Avatar. The Northern Water Tribe survived the war with very little loss by keeping the Earth Kingdom between them and the Fire Nation. The Earth Kingdom made the correct military assumption that they could outlast the Fire Nation by virtue of being larger and more rich in resources to withstand the Fire Nation's military industrialization. There's not really a lot of either colony building by the Fire Nation, or moving resources from local populations to the Fire Nation.<br /> <br /> So, to me, the Fire Nation's war of conquest resembles far more the Napoleonic Wars. France wasn't some unknown superpower, but through a combination of factors managed to take on almost every other major power in Europe at the time; largely because the Coalition couldn't actually keep itself together and all to often it turned into every country for themselves before the French were actually stopped. If the Earth Kingdom and Water Tribes actually worked together, they could have resisted the Fire Nation far more effectively and potentially put a stop to their ambitions before the Comet would have been close. Likewise if the Coalition took France's advances more seriously, and worked together more completely, they could have won far more decisively and earlier.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Nobody would say the native americans wanting to reject the change brougth by europeans it's Capitalist Realism, and certainly I can't see how that applies to Legend of Aang political arena </div> </div> </div>It's kind of a strawman no? You can't see how your addition of Native Americans as a comparison doesn't apply? I wasn't talking about Native Americans, or using them as part of my point. Aang wasn't fighting to reject Fire Nation colonialism, he was fighting to stop the Fire Nation from completely eliminating the balance of the Four Nations; which he restored.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There wasn't much Capitalism to upend in LoA world, in the first place - and that it's quite an important element in such a concept, as the name would indicate </div> </div> </div>It seems to me the Imperialist Realism would have been just the same in cultural imagination.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Because this change it's obviously a good thing, it's the closest to such a concept as Capitalist Realism I can think off in LoA (as in, the world <i>obviously </i>has to evolve in that direction - but that's a very small thing). </div> </div> </div>But the shows never portray this as an &quot;obviously a good thing&quot; fashion. The Republic is one city, and its values are not showing to have spread beyond in a significant degree. That city is rife with turmoil and the changes wrought brought on lots of problems that spark a traditionalist backlash and new problems arise. Where does the implication that the world &quot;obviously&quot; have to evolve in that direction get displayed in the setting... when the world shows no inclination of doing so?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1446549#post1446549\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Very true but nobility doesn't = Bender either, even if in this world you'd think it would.</div> </div> </div> </div>Aristocracy isn't limited to nobility.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Earth King and Queen isn't necessarily a Bender, for example. Indeed, they very often seem not to be. </div> </div> </div>Yes, but they're shown to be rather weak monarchs that are practically if not overtly puppets of the powerful Benders that actually run the country.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Its also not at all relevant to the Air Nomads because every Air Nomad was a bender and they practiced communal nomadism with no accumulation of wealth whatsoever but shared property. </div> </div> </div>I don't know... the tautology of all Air Nomads being Air Benders implies that anyone of Air Nomad descent that isn't a Bender is inherently incapable of being part of their birth culture...that's not really all that great if you think about it. Either the Air Nomads were ridiculously perfect in passing on their spiritual teachings such that every child of their people embraced it and none of them ever failed to become an Air Bender for it, or the Air Nomads didn't have an underclass of non-Benders because they effectively exiled them instead<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The belief is that an anarchist's purpose is to cast down corrupt systems and then THE PEOPLE AS A WHOLE will decide on creating a fairer and more just system. </div> </div> </div>And in the specific individualist strain of anarchism Zaheer practices, &quot;just systems&quot; are inherently ad hoc ones that only exist while mutually beneficial for the involved parties and nobody can be forced into one or forced to stay in one if they no longer agree with the validity of the system's necessity.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T22:19:26-07:00","id":1446560,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1446554#post1446554\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So the resentment and hatred of Benders comes at the time when they're the most useless.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is fairly in keeping with human nature. Oppositional movements frequently gain their most strength when there's a perception of weakness in the power of their perceived others. It's a lot easier to convince people to stand up against a government that seems weak and ineffectual as it both reinforces the belief in a need to replace it and emboldens people that would not normally participate in such political actions to do so with less fear of reprisals.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T22:27:40-07:00","id":1446561,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1446559#post1446559\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And in the specific individualist strain of anarchism Zaheer practices, &quot;just systems&quot; are inherently ad hoc ones that only exist while mutually beneficial for the involved parties and nobody can be forced into one or forced to stay in one if they no longer agree with the validity of the system's necessity.</div> </div> </div> </div>Zaheer is also correct about numerous things:<br /> <br /> * Monarchy is a ridiculous system that has no reason to continue to be practiced when democratic systems exist.<br /> * The Avatar is often a force of conservativism and stasis.<br /> * The division between Earth, Air, Fire, and Water nations is partially due to the avatar when there's no reason that there should be 4 Turtle-descended nations versus hundreds of them like almost exist in the Earth Kingdom.<br /> * The Avatar is overly relied upon when, as Zaheer points out, they have the individual power to change things themselves.<br /> * Zaheer as the &quot;Sokka&quot; of their group is their leader not because of his lack of powers [prior to his empowering] but because of his mental acuity. Thus bending is not necessary to lead or rule.<br /> <br /> There's also a decent argument that Zaheer's philosophy of &quot;destruction and see what happens&quot; actually works with Kuvira. The problem is Zaheer didn't think the circumstances that would emerge from the anarchic nonfunctional chaos of the Earth Kingdom would be to bounce the other direction to a highly centralized military and cultural dictatorship.<br /> <br /> Society definitely reformed and had the technology as well as resources to be a superpower. It's just that it was about to become Fire Nation 2.0.<br /> <br /> Zaheer's failure is he missed that &quot;The People&quot; can be assholes too and would gladly follow a Strong WomanTM like Kuvira.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":12002,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","user":"CTPhipps","date":". 04-30-2021, 01:31 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-30T03:43:48-07:00","id":1446580,"title":"","content":"I think it's also fair to say that Zaheer's (justifiable) ignorance of exactly how fast technology was advancing impacted his miscalculations a lot. Even two years earlier in the setting, and Kuvira's ability to project force would have been so much less in scope, that it would have been unlikely for her to really be able to demonstrate her &quot;strength&quot; to so many so fast.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-30T05:58:38-07:00","id":1446589,"title":"","content":"So...<br /> <br /> Are we going to say that the image of Zaheer killing the queen, and then the whole city immediately catching fire...it's not a bad portrayal of Anarchism?<br /> <br /> I love that you can defend Zaheer's intentions by making a case he had a lot of good points (and I agree) and he just failed by being too idealistic, but the immediate image the whole gig gives to me it's that Anarchism = Fire &amp; Chaos. A popular image that anarchists have been figthing a lot.<br /> <br /> I grant that such being the genuine intentions of some anarchists can prove Zaheer it's an Anarchist, for what it's worth. I don't think it's worth a lot - it's still a pretty bad portrayal, as far as portraying a political ideology as a whole (it's never stated that Zaheer it's a very specifik kind of anarchist, and not all anarchists support the whole &quot;chaos &amp; setting everything on fire&quot; thing. He is THE anarchist).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-30T06:25:42-07:00","id":1446592,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1446580#post1446580\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think it's also fair to say that Zaheer's (justifiable) ignorance of exactly how fast technology was advancing impacted his miscalculations a lot. Even two years earlier in the setting, and Kuvira's ability to project force would have been so much less in scope, that it would have been unlikely for her to really be able to demonstrate her &amp;quot;strength&amp;quot; to so many so fast.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>That makes sense considering he was imprisoned for who knows how long","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23952-konradleijon","name":"Konradleijon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23952_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1441"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-30T09:22:55-07:00","id":1446615,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1446559#post1446559\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">We can have a discourse about the politics of LoK without making it an unjustified choice between two mutually exclusive options. So... I don't know what aesthetics or discourse in making this uncritically about communism vs. fascism.</div> </div> </div> </div>Nobody say uncritically. That's just how the debate started in a different thread, if you go back to the 1rst post that's what I said.<br /> <br /> The way I expressed myself latter, when changing towards Communism vs Facism (to respect your position about Communism and Socialism) may have been unfortunate, but my intentions never changed since the beggining of the thread. It was just miscomunication.<br /> <br /> And the aesthetics and discourse are those the show uses to depict the Equalists, the video you find more compelling says these are populist aesthetics, also shows how there are many paralels with the aesthetic and methods of Communist propaganda (the video just says this isn't enough reason to call it Comunist, because Facists used it too). Hence why, I imagine, one would feel inclined to think about these when watching Amon - this is political messaging rigth here<br /> <br /> The political message of a show isn't wath the characters believe (and perhaps not what the authors believe, either), it's what the show <b><i>shows</i></b><br /> <br /> And about them being mutually exclusive...I'm wondering if they need to be.<br /> <br /> If we go to authoral intent:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> After ATLA ended we had the idea for a revolutionary sect of non-bending Chi-blockers. Once we started development on TLOK and had the setting of a modern metropolis in the Avatar world we thought that concept was a good fit. The age-old tensions between those who had bending abilities and those who didn\u2019t would be brought into sharp contrast in this huge, melting pot of a city. Even though our fantasy world celebrates the amazing powers of benders, we wanted to take a look at how these societies had developed with a bias towards benders and how that has impacted those without bending. As for developing Tarrlok and Amon, the real world has taught us that no matter how noble a cause is, there will always be individuals who will exploit it.<br /> ...<br /> We created ATLA and TLOK as series that would stand the test of time and deal with subjects and issues that people and societies have always struggled with. </div> </div> </div>Now, that's very vague. Some words, like revolutionaries and social bias, ring a bell - but ultimately these words can imply many things. Like, social bias it's never very good, but one could question &quot;how much&quot; and if it's really that big of a deal. And there are many kinds of revolutions ... and all that serves for the proposition I'm going to make below<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">And... given the Western bias of creators of the show, the audience of the show, and the users of this board.... communist and fascist don't have derogatory connotations?<br /> <br /> Populism is a far more accurate term. Given how charged most of these terms are, accuracy feels more important to me.</div> </div> </div> </div>Populism it's a term that could refer to both Fascism and Communism, and then some<i>: What if instead of either or neiter, Amon represents BOTH? </i><br /> <br /> While IRL these are opposite ideologies, a single symbol can be interpreted as having more than one connotation. And that does seem to jive with the intent of the authors - since they wanted to talk about things that would stand the test of time, and that would imply that you should be able to apply the message to more than one thing.<br /> <br /> I think it's interesting as an analisis to explore how these &quot;subjects and issues that people and societies have always struggled with&quot; relate with the, <i>as per Heavy Arms</i>, &quot;political upheavals of the early 1900s&quot; that one, as a viewer not knowing the intentions of the authors, could read as a political message<br /> <br /> Now, neither fits like a shoe with the Equalists - but, for the purposes of analizing political connotations, they don't need to fit so neatly. That Communism doesn't talk about inherent cualities being good or bad, or Facism doesn't talk so much about equality for all. In other words: That &quot;Amon it's neither&quot; - I would take as factual. BUT one could interpret this as a hint or a cautionary tale that can apply to either, or both, as the big Populist movements of the XX century.<br /> <br /> I my opinion, the words<i> revolutionaries</i> figthing against<i> bias towards a part of society</i> (aka. to defend marginalized people) sounds more socialist than facist. But I can't ignore the similarities in some of the opinions (like with bending as &quot;impurities&quot;, the class of the opressors being defined by a natural trait) with Facism. Since both were a big deal in the XX century, I think now Amon can be understood as a reference to both <hr /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It does seem strange to attempt to analyze the politics of a show without taking the entirety of the show into account. While nothing recontextualzies Amon in a significant fashion later, the point of the different antagonists and Korra's reactions to them is written as a more cohesive statement. </div> </div> </div>It's a limitation, as I can only mention facts from the first 3 books, but that's where the rest of the forum can educate me.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm not sure what the value of the concept is to this discussion if it can't be applied beyond the strict realms of a capitalist society. Capitalism, even by the time of LoK, is in its infancy in the setting. It's neither traditional or the status quo of the world in the story. If the authors have literally penned a world where capitalism has barely arisen and seems like it is at significant risk of not spreading... it doesn't seem like it can apply to the authors either.<br /> <br /> The concept of &quot;it's easier to picture the end of the world than picture something different from our default status quo,&quot; doesn't seem something uniquely capitalist to me, and removed to the level of a more natural human inclination, </div> </div> </div>The idea that LoK projects a vision of Capitalist Realims doesn't need to imply that the setting it's strictly or egemonically Capitalist. Capitalist Realism implicates that Capitalism, even if it's super flawed, it's the only way. That other solutions are either worse or just not possible<br /> <br /> But don't take my word for it: I recomend everyone to go and read the book called that. It's short and very well regarded in leftist circles. And that way you won't depend on my personal take on the concept, you migth even conclude I'm misrepresenting what Mark Fisher said. I hope I'm not, but I'm far from perfect.<br /> <br /> I think that a setting where Capitalism it's nascent and/or menaced it's the perfect setting to make a story of Capitalist Realism. You can paint the meances and older systems as an evil, that needs to be destroyed. You can pain bourgeoisie as good, or at least necessary. You can state that, even if capitalism it's no paradise, we should defend it against all that are against it. Because it's <i>better</i>. And inside the Lore this will be true...but as a political message, it can be pretty bad.<br /> <br /> What you can't take from Capitalist Realism it's the Capitalist word. One can talk a lot about the historical tendency of people thinking they current way of life it's natural and eternal. But I think that if you just grab Capitalist Realism to do that, you neuter it's importance as a political statement about the current world. And thus also neuter any<i> messages about RL politics</i> it can have for Korra or any other series.<br /> <br /> Also, I think there was a misunderstanding somewhere. Prob. my fault. I wasn't talking about Capitalist Realism as a problem Korra has as a chatacter, but rather as an (unintended) political message that LoK seems to convey.<br /> <br /> Now, I don't think this can be <i>factualy</i> proven - because I don't think it's really falsable by any other thing than Capitalism dying and being replaced by a better system in the Lore - but I do have reasons to suspect:<br /> <br /> *Republic City (the cradle of capitalism) status quo being always restored after the defeat of the villans that at least said they were trying to change the status quo for the better (other places, however, can be revolutionized - like Ba Sing Se, albeit that's giving a mixed message, with all the city burning and all).<br /> <br /> *That we know that, intentions of the villans aside, they never represented better alternatives (If Zaheer Anarchism was supposed to be the better alternative to anything but the Earth Kingdom's Feudal tyrant, it's not very well shown. I understand that caused Facism, and Republic City's political ideology of democracy &amp; freedom it's shown to be the way, not Anarchism - but correct me if I'm wrong, as I haven't seen that part).<br /> <br /> * Varrick, arguably the main representative of the evils of Capitalism, it's - by the music, the animation, and also how he gets punished - presented in a much more sympathetic light than the rest of the villains.<br /> <br /> If there's a difference in book 4, feel free to make a case about it. The video I saw shows he got off the hook of substantially helping the dictator w/o major consequences, and got comically redeemed w/o dying - which everyone just accepts. How does Zaheer redemption goes?, is he free and pursuing anarchism now?. I would take that as a case of season 4 being less adamant about the good of alternative political systems<br /> <br /> *The social problems of Republic City are never shown in the same bad ligth as the problems in other - less capitalistic - places. Like with how missery get's romantized in Republic City with that happy hobo, and never showing much else about poverty - not so romantized in Ba Sing Se, where they show they eat rotten fruit, and talk about it as a bad thing.<br /> <br /> *You could even expand this to other capitalistic places and characters:<br /> <br /> Like with Beifong's sister. She was a pirate that left her life of crime aside, bougth a land and built a city Now she doesn't bad things and has family, thus she good, no reason to dwell on the past . She's also the owner of a city &quot;that embodies progress, innovation, and advancement in a plethora of fields&quot;.<br /> She's super good, and if you think she's not so good because she never really paid for her crimes, and because she disfigured Lin, and wasn't even able to show an apologetic reation after metting her (instead implying Lin it's in the wrong for not letting bygones be bygones) - then, man, YOU have a problem...<br /> <br /> Or with Asami's father - If my memory doesn't fail, <s>he was helping Amon, but he wasn't <i>reeeealy</i> as bad as Amon and most of his followers. He just believed the good parts about equality and all that, but doesn't relate anymore after he sees Amon's evils. </s> Ok, my memory WAS failing. He was a very convinced Equalist, and a pretty bad person. He does pay a whole lot more than Varrick.<br /> I take that as a valid counterpoint - the thing is I don't see where he's supposed to be<i> worse, </i>other than siding with Amon's lot for a grudge, instead of doing the same for proffit (by itself, I don't see it as a a a very strong counterpoint).<br /> <br /> *What about anti-capitalistic places and characters?, those that would defy or refuse to follow Republic City's ideologies? They seem to be either apolitical, bigots/tyrants, or very wrong about very fundamental things.<br /> <br /> You have the absolutely horrible Queen of Earth. There's the not-so-bad, but still pretty damn bad, Zaheer (let's remember that he personaly tortured Korra, among other many bad things - and where I'm aiming it's at what the show <i>shows</i>).<br /> <br /> Anything I forget, Is there some enemy of the status quo of Republic City that would be depicted in a more positive ligth, w/o being politicaly inefectual?.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> it seems justified to phrase Aang's reversion of thing to the status quo from before his imprisonment as this sort of thinking. </div> </div> </div>Aang doesn't care about changing the status quo, just about defeating &quot;the bad guy&quot; and &quot;restoring balance&quot;.<br /> <br /> However, that doesn't make it a LoA general statement about any RL political ideology, capitalist or otherwise. Thus I don't see your reconceptualization as very useful here.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It's kind of a strawman no? You can't see how your addition of Native Americans as a comparison doesn't apply? </div> </div> </div>It does seem a pretty apt comparison for the Northen Water Tribe.<br /> <br /> But the intention was to showcase that you can't just put ALL the &quot;returns to the status quo&quot; in the same basket. It's different when the status quo it's doing the oppressing than when it's being attacked by an external, conquering, force.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I wasn't talking about Native Americans, or using them as part of my point. Aang wasn't fighting to reject Fire Nation colonialism, he was fighting to stop the Fire Nation from completely eliminating the balance of the Four Nations; which he restored. </div> </div> </div>The Fire Nation was in the process of conquering the world, and the assesment from the Earth Nation was wrong in many, many parts, as the taking of Ba Sing Se and the whole comet thing shows. Also, they were very much making colonies: <a href=\"https://avatar.fandom.com/wiki/Fire_Nation_colonies\" target=\"_blank\">https://avatar.fandom.com/wiki/Fire_Nation_colonies </a><br /> <br /> That the war is shown more as mere destruction than colonization it's just a technicism. In the same vein, Avatar isn't a series about economy, I wouldn't ask them to depict resources being moved. If you know about real life colonies, you could expect a similar agenda<br /> <br /> In fact, I view the attempted eradication of many culturaly distinct groups of people along the way (to stop the cycle of the Avatar, if you want, it doesn't matter for the political message) should say something about some similar real world process ... I see that as Imperialist England because they were the most industrialized empire of the time, and part of their rethoric was that they should share all that civilization with the rest of the world. But one could equally talk about Imperial France, or Nazis even. It's a very universal message.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But the shows never portray this as an &quot;obviously a good thing&quot; fashion. The Republic is one city, and its values are not showing to have spread beyond in a significant degree. That city is rife with turmoil and the changes wrought brought on lots of problems that spark a traditionalist backlash and new problems arise. Where does the implication that the world &quot;obviously&quot; have to evolve in that direction get displayed in the setting... when the world shows no inclination of doing so? </div> </div> </div>Well, you don't see that.<br /> <br /> But I see the happy hobo of shanty town romantizing poverty under Capitalism, whereas in the Earth Kingdom poor people it's shown in a more negative ligth. I see the capitalist order in Republic City being menaced by forces of change that spouse progressist ideals, but are undisputably evil - and thus returning to that capitalistic status quo as the only reasonable, even if inherently flawed, option.I see Anarchy being very wrong about some very crucial things about people, and that inevitably leading towards Fascism. I see the Capitalist villan portrayed as much more sympathetic than the rest of the villans.<br /> <br /> I see a lot more problems with that than with the fact Aang restoring the Status Quo that was being broken by the imperialistic overlords of the Fire Nation ...and thus he was not a progressist (which apparenlty it's supposed to be a bad thing regardless of the material conditions, and regardless of what the conclusion says about some real world politics?)<br /> <br /> And I don't care if it was intentional or not. I just see that message rigtht there, and will need more than small details to chage opinion. Maybe that HUGE change to the political status quo of the world Korra did at the end of book 4 could convince me?. What did she do, exactly?<br /> <br /> Note that I don't deny the other messages you speak about - the fact you see other messages, doesn't mean the one I see isn't there too as a valid interpretation. A work can have many different interpretations","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 04-30-2021, 03:56 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-30T15:55:45-07:00","id":1446676,"title":"","content":"Honestly, I'm losing sight of the point of all this. It really feels like you decided before any actually critical analysis of the work that it was all about that it was a pro-capitalist screed so you could post anti-capitalist critiques at whatever data points you want to cherry pick. This does not feel like a good faith debate about figuring out what the political messages if LoK are, but rather railing on the ones you want to claim are there without actually having to prove it.<br /> <br /> The whole &quot;what if Amon is both?&quot; is a perfect encapsulation of this mentality. The authors can't 'just be talking about revolutionary populism, because there were violent revolutionary populists that were pro-capitalism too (like... the founders of the USA!). You need the discussion around Amon to center around juxtaposing fascism and communism because in the middle of those? (Neo-)Liberal capitalism. That makes Amon &quot;not-capitalist&quot; and the main characters/Republic City &quot;capitalist&quot; by default. It's just a way to manufacture a political point instead of proving that it's there.<br /> <br /> If you want to have a debate about political ideologies... we can go have a thread about that. If you actually care about analyzing the political messages of LoK, lets have a real discussion about what those are, not what we want them to be to justify criticisms that don't seem to actually be about what's in the show.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-30T18:21:30-07:00","id":1446710,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1446676#post1446676\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The whole &quot;what if Amon is both?&quot; is a perfect encapsulation of this mentality. The authors can't 'just be talking about revolutionary populism, because there were violent revolutionary populists that were pro-capitalism too (like... the founders of the USA!).</div> </div> </div> </div>It's a fair point, I reckon that <i>both </i>doesn't work very well.<br /> <br /> It was a dumb idea, that's leaving aside other posibilities (like the founders of USA, I never tought about that, and &quot;not being represented&quot; was one of the grievances there), and ultimately doesn't serve any purpose that Populism wouldn't do better.<br /> <br /> It's not both, it's <i>Populism</i>.<br /> <br /> Which it's far more ample than both, can and does include some of the historical failings of both, but can be more than that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You need the discussion around Amon to center around juxtaposing fascism and communism because in the middle of those? (Neo-)Liberal capitalism. That makes Amon &quot;not-capitalist&quot; and the main characters/Republic City &quot;capitalist&quot; by default. It's just a way to manufacture a political point instead of proving that it's there. </div> </div> </div>I...didn't put Among among the &quot;anty-capitalistic villans&quot; precisely because one can make a case about Amon not being anty-capitalist at all (he would be, if he was just representing Communism, but Facism isn't anty-capitalist. That boat sailed away, even before I realized how dumb was <i>&quot;both&quot; </i>instead of just Populism).<br /> <br /> We don't know how Amon plans to organize economy (assuming a plan), but the presence of Asami's father it's a potential indication of a capitalistic Populism.<br /> <br /> (Like Facism was...? Sorry, I'm not saying it's Facism. But the more I think about it the more merit I see on that idea. Still think Populism can tackle the issues of Facisms better, non the least because a populist movement with legitimate grievances causes a lot less heartache than explicit facists with legitimate grievances)<br /> <br /> I'm not manufacturing shit with Amon, but I grant my take on the politics he's representing was flawed.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Honestly, I'm losing sight of the point of all this. It really feels like you decided before any actually critical analysis of the work that it was all about that it was a pro-capitalist screed so you could post anti-capitalist critiques at whatever data points you want to cherry pick. This does not feel like a good faith debate about figuring out what the political messages if LoK are, but rather railing on the ones you want to claim are there without actually having to prove it. </div> </div> </div>I decided nothing.<br /> <br /> I can't unsee Varrick getting away with everything he does with just a slap on the wrist.<br /> <br /> I can't usee all the things I said I saw at the end of my, admitedly far too large, post. From the city catching fire the moment an anarchist takes power, to the fact all forces of change that declare progressist ideals end being undisputably evil, or bad (you can include Amon's Populists here)<br /> <br /> I can accept I was wrong about Amon being a Communist, I can see the merit of seeing him as a Facist, and I can see it's better to say he's <i>neither</i>...<br /> but, I can't just concede the show hasn't any kind of pro-capitalist message, with how they portray the different characters and their agendas<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If you want to have a debate about political ideologies... we can go have a thread about that. If you actually care about analyzing the political messages of LoK, lets have a real discussion about what those are, not what we want them to be to justify criticisms that don't seem to actually be about what's in the show. </div> </div> </div>Sorry, but I can't accept that what I saw in the show <i>wasn't in the show</i><br /> <br /> My interpretation about these points could be flawed, but you're simply wrong at stating such an interpretation isn't about what's in the show<br /> <br /> I don't know what you think a <i>message</i> is, but I'm far from the only one that saw such a message there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 04-30-2021, 09:53 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-30T20:06:21-07:00","id":1446722,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1446710#post1446710\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">but, I can't just concede the show hasn't any kind of pro-capitalist message, with how they portray the different characters and their agendas</div> </div> </div> </div>But that's the entire problem. You're not looking at the context of the totality of the story, you're picking the things that scream out to your perceptions as pro-capitalist and then taking that to be the end of the analysis.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Sorry, but I can't accept that what I saw in the show <i>wasn't in the show</i> </div> </div> </div>I'm not asking you to. I'm asking you to go through the process of re-evaluating the labels that apply to Amon with the whole story. I'm not saying you're going to reach the same conclusions as me, but I'm asking you to engage with the process of literary analysis rather than jumping to the conclusion.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't know what you think a <i>message</i> is, but I'm far from the only one that saw such a message there. </div> </div> </div>The problem is, messages are communication. There's intent from the speaker, the noise generated in the flaws of how communication is expressed, what the receiver perceives, and how the receiver interprets that jumble of inputs.<br /> <br /> And... it doesn't seem like I'm the only one that's gotten the <u>message</u> from you I expressed in my previous post.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-01T11:01:08-07:00","id":1446812,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1446722#post1446722\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But that's the entire problem. You're not looking at the context of the totality of the story, you're picking the things that scream out to your perceptions as pro-capitalist and then taking that to be the end of the analysis.</div> </div> </div> </div>I can't look at the totality because I haven's seen book 4. And there's where you have a grerat chance to correct me and/or the videos I posted: Yet you're tremendously vague about that final choice to eliminate the &quot;status quo&quot; Korra makes at the end. The way you portray it, it may not have much to do with making a political change, the way the villans were attempting. But please, be more specific, and correct my general impresion up to book 3.<br /> <br /> Also I think we should analize why Varrick it's the way it is. He's not just a capitalist and a villan, but a villan whose motives are capitalist. That was the great chance for the show the evils of Capitalism - and I think the show <i>does</i> try to showcase those evils in Varrick's numerous misdeeds. Yet he's the most likeable of all villains, by far. It's treated as a &quot;funny&quot; character, and gets off the hock rather easily.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm not asking you to. I'm asking you to go through the process of re-evaluating the labels that apply to Amon with the whole story </div> </div> </div>Already did<br /> <br /> I can see why many people would see Amon the way I did. You did made a comparison with Stalin and Mao at the beginning of the thread, so I imagine you can see some paralels too - but I can also see applying such a strict label to someone that doesn't quite fit, and can fit other labels too, may be a wrong way to go about it. I think that's missing the forest for the trees, and will only impoverish the analysis.<br /> <br /> I think you're focusing too much on Amon not being a Comminist caricature made by someone who doesn't understand comunism (my initial statement), but rather a more general critic about Populisms - as if that proved the <i>rest </i>of the analisis, that covers 3 seasons and a plethora of characters, it's all wrong.<br /> <br /> IF Amon was such a caricature, that would add more merit to the analisis, both mine and the one in the videos I posted<br /> <br /> BUT we're taliking about 4 videos here, and a lot of characters and situations I mentioned that have nothing to do with that villan. Focusing so much about Amon like you're doing now, and also in your previous post (in spite of the fact that I separated the part with Amon from the rest of the post, and barely mentioned him afterwards - <i>in a counterpoint</i> <i>to my own idea</i>). Isn't that kind like cherry picking?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The problem is, messages are communication. There's intent from the speaker, the noise generated in the flaws of how communication is expressed, what the receiver perceives, and how the receiver interprets that jumble of inputs. </div> </div> </div>*The intent from the speaker, as I said, probably <i>wasn't </i>what crafted the message I see.<br /> <br /> *The points I checked are what I see as the noise and flaws of the communication, that end crafting a message that most likely wasn't intended.<br /> <br /> *What I perceived was an intent to make a point about how the status quo it's flawed (hence why all the villans &quot;have a point&quot;, and apparenly Korra agrees) , but, because of the flaws on the communication, ends being a message about how all the people that seek extreme social change are wrong. How the occidental capitalism of Republic City it's not great, but it's the best we have.<br /> <br /> I see that inserting itself into a larger meta-narrative about ppl seeking to change the status quo being evil in spite of them having a point, that can be seen on countless shows, and I relate with Capitalist Realism<br /> <br /> This is a valid way of analisis. A message doesn't always come from intent. Death of the Author, and all that. I think you pointed out that I souldn't try to infer what the author was thinking from what I see in the show. And correctly.so.<br /> <br /> My analisis would be complete if I could include book 4, but I'm not going to star watching a series I don't like (and not just for it's politics) just to make a point. If book 4 proves I'm wrong, kudos.<br /> <br /> I will accept your aguments as long as :<br /> *They aren't small details (like a character not being explicit about <i>economics and international relations</i>, in a show that isn't mainly about that. A show that has glossed over much more important details, as can be seen in <i>CTPhipps </i>arguments against nonbenders suffering from social bias)<br /> *Can't be disproved by a simple Wiky search.<br /> *And if they engage with the argument (Saying Zaheer and the other villans are a critic against the &quot;great man&quot; argument of LoA it's a valid analisis, and I think it's correct, but isn't really engeging with <i>my </i>argument - perhaps you should expand on that in a sepparate post that isn't an answer, that way your analisis isn't going to get jumbled with a criticism of mine)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And... it doesn't seem like I'm the only one that's gotten the <u>message</u> from you I expressed in my previous post. </div> </div> </div>Let's not fall into ad hominem. Criticize my way of analizing, if you want, but not my &quot;mentality&quot;. Shall we?<br /> <br /> That's wasn't a direct insult, but it's rather close and I think you need to stop going on about it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-01-2021, 02:57 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-01T15:31:30-07:00","id":1446854,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1446812#post1446812\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I can't look at the totality because I haven's seen book 4.</div> </div> </div> </div>What's the point of trying to have a conversation about the politics of show if you haven't actually watched all of it? While we could discuss the flaws in individual Books and all, they don't exist in a vacuum. As a sequel LoK's political statements have to be considered in the light of TLA's political statements; they can't be completely severed. Likewise we can't completely sever anything in a specific Book from the total story.<br /> <br /> I think a constructive political literary analysis of LoK needs to start with what the show is trying to say; what themes and morals it states through its presentation. Then we can look at the flaws in how that's communicated, and have a solid discussion about how real world political ideologies might have influenced those flaws. I don't see the productive avenue of hunting around the shows for, &quot;this is about capitalism, that's about about anarchism, this other things is about fascism, and this other things is about communism.&quot; Even characters like Varrick and Zaheer who are directly stated to personally hold to a political identity like that doesn't mean the show is trying, or even does incidentally, offer a critique of those ideologies. That doesn't make it good. not making a statement is frequently worse than making an iffy statement.<br /> <br /> Your frustration with the tolerance of Varrick's complete amorality getting a pass is justified, but it's a muddied message because the show treats him as wrong in that regard, but not irredeemable despite him showing no real signs of change; just signs of modulating his behavior to better fit in with the good guys. The fact that the show is not as strongly condemning of Varrick as Amon doesn't mean the show treat's Varrick's behaviors as good. So... is that pro-capitalist? Not really. Is it anti-capitalist? Not effectively because of the muddying the show does around it. Thus the question: is Varrick's character really about capitalism just because he's a capitalist (its hardly his only demographic or cultural identifier too)? Or like Amon, is he a commentary on a broader topic than a specific -ism? Varrick's biggest personal flaw is that he's extremely subject to the desire to be the One Great Man of his age. He ignores the morality of his choices over the social impact of his name slapped on anything. He ignores the people that have made his rise to success possible to perpetuate a self and public image of a &quot;self-made man,&quot; which bites him in the ass, a lot (and is the catalyst for what little change he manages).<br /> <br /> So, is there value in looking at him in the lens of &quot;critique of capitalism? Y/N?&quot; versus looking at him as a critique of the myth of the Great Industrialists That Built Your Nation? Yes capitalism is inherently part of that, but it isn't the sole core of if the show pulls of that critique or not; the capitalist version of this subset of the Great Man thing is a continuation of a cultural trend that predates capitalism or the Industrial Revolution.<br /> <br /> There's also a major thematic core to include in that LoK is intended to be an exploration of the construction of personal identities. Varrick is, in that light, a critique of trying to &quot;fake it till you make it,&quot; more than anything else (also something that had a bit of prominence in the 20s as well as the more recent take on it, playing into the authors desires to focus on things that feel like timeless struggles). Varrick fakes it, makes it, but ingrains that mentality into his identity to the point where he's obsessed with making it &quot;more&quot; somehow which only leads to negative results.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Yet he's the most likeable of all villains, by far. It's treated as a &quot;funny&quot; character, and gets off the hock rather easily. </div> </div> </div>He's the most relatable of all the antagonists because his desires and motivations are ones that are the most common among the audience. I find him obnoxious, grating, and I would take a decade of having philosophic debates with Zaheer over having to spend five minutes in a room with Varrick.<br /> <br /> Of course, why does Varrick get off easily? Because he's useful to people. It's a great condemnation of mostly Republic City (in your estimation the bastion of capitalism...) and Kuvira that he's constantly forgiven by leadership because he's too useful to keep locked up. He is openly aware of the fact that he can get away with almost anything because he's considered too useful of a tool to people with political power for them to ever dispose of him (even if it means switching sides, a lot). It seems to me an extremely valid criticism of society that Varrick is a &quot;villain&quot; because he's learned that society will tolerate his crimes if he does them &quot;right,&quot; unlike the other bad guys. History is replete with examples of this, and its not something humanity has really figured out how to adequately deal with.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And there's where you have a grerat chance to correct me and/or the videos I posted: </div> </div> </div>It's not my job to educate you. Also &quot;reject&quot; not &quot;eliminate.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Already did </div> </div> </div>Then you're not really expressing this in a process I can appreciate.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I think you're focusing too much on Amon not being a Comminist caricature made by someone who doesn't understand comunism (my initial statement), but rather a more general critic about Populisms - as if that proved the <i>rest </i>of the analisis, that covers 3 seasons and a plethora of characters, it's all wrong. </div> </div> </div>The specific video's linked to by you certainly lost me as criticism worth spending time watching because of this issue, but in the thread I don't really feel that's a fair assessment. I happily moved on to talking about Zaheer. I engaged with debates over the wider scale assessment of the geopolitical nature of the 100 year war, with the nature of Bending as a source of privilege, and so on. You're brought Amon back up after he wasn't the focus of the conversation, not me. I'm not shoving him into every post, and at least after post #20 I had moved on from focusing my points on him, or even the Equalists, until you tried out that whole &quot;what about both&quot; thing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Isn't that kind like cherry picking? </div> </div> </div>&quot;Isn't that kind [of] like...&quot; is a very telling phrasing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What I perceived was an intent to make a point about how the status quo it's flawed (hence why all the villans &quot;have a point&quot;, and apparenly Korra agrees) , but, because of the flaws on the communication, ends being a message about how all the people that seek extreme social change are wrong. How the occidental capitalism of Republic City it's not great, but it's the best we have. </div> </div> </div>The problem is, the show also presents that the mindset of those that want to maintain the status quo are also wrong. The show is more sympathetic to them because their methods are more sympathetic in general (like... trying to fix things without killing lots of people is more sympathetic than trying to fix things by killing a lot of people). The show has a clear negative view of both extremists and conservatives (in the classic sense, not the modern US political sense). While the show does not offer a better solution (making it hard to place where it's politics really land in the broad spectrum available), it does not rest on the idea that Republic City is the best we have. There's a clear message that it's the best they have, and it still needs to get better. That last part is something you seem to refuse to want to acknowledge. So you can assert:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I see that inserting itself into a larger meta-narrative about ppl seeking to change the status quo being evil... </div> </div> </div>And then talk about Capital Realism (despite evidence it doesn't apply because the show sees the need to look for better options not just stick with what's current the best option). The show's total message is not &quot;people seeking to change the status quo are evil,&quot; but that changing the status quo without making things worse is extremely hard and those that tell you otherwise are wrong. The show wants you to seek out a better way of doing things by affirming that you can reject the status quo in a constructive fashion (that's essentially Korra whole character arc).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I think you pointed out that I souldn't try to infer what the author was thinking from what I see in the show. And correctly.so. </div> </div> </div>That doesn't mean completely embracing Death of the Author (because Death of the Author is an extremely blunt and limited analytic tool). You can account for the authors without trying to make assumptions about what's going on in their minds purely based on your interpretations of their work; esp. in this case since they have expressed their feelings for public consumption.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I will accept your aguments as long as : </div> </div> </div>Eh... sometimes I really wonder how I have such a strong reputation around here and yet people pull this sort of stuff on me like part of my reputation isn't my habit of blasting this sort of &quot;my way or the highway&quot; argumentation.<br /> <br /> If you want to have a discussion, we can have a discussion, and either my points will be informative for you or they wouldn't. I have no interest in the emotional labor of dealing with a thread where I have to meet a list of demands to have my points acknowledged.<br /> <br /> However emphatically I made my feelings known, I didn't demand you not treat socialism and communism as the same thing, I question it, and you respected that and conceded the point. I have no idea why you wouldn't at least return the gesture in hopes of reciprocation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Let's not fall into ad hominem. Criticize my way of analizing, if you want, but not my &quot;mentality&quot;. Shall we? </div> </div> </div>But I didn't say anything about your mentality. I said something about your message, and how it's being interpreted. I vaguely hoped you might see the parallels in how much you didn't like my comment with your own comments towards the &quot;messages&quot; in LoK, but I'm not surprised that didn't work either.<br /> <br /> As far as the whole ad hominem thing... yeah? You're doing it too, because we've moved to talking about each other (ad hominem, to the man, as in not to the debate). When I posted my whole &quot;losing the point of the thread thing,&quot; you could have recalibrated and recentered... but where we are instead. If you want me to stop talking about this stuff, post other stuff to talk about.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-02T06:02:41-07:00","id":1446960,"title":"","content":"The biggest problem with that whole arc was that the anger and resentment Amon harnessed seems to have come out of nowhere. For it to be like the alt-right or similar fascist movements, he'd need to be using the aggrieved entitlement of a priviledged class who aren't getting the world they were always promised<br /> <br /> Like that's not how either fascism and communist works","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23952-konradleijon","name":"Konradleijon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23952_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1441"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-02T15:46:11-07:00","id":1447052,"title":"","content":"It is, however, fairly consistant with populism in the face of sudden democracy.<br /> <br /> One of the failings of how various countries and organizations have tried to spread democracy (and specifically liberal democracy) around the world is that suddenly adding democracy to a culture pretty much doesn't work. It tends to just destabilize a country and let strong man dictator of whatever stripe whip up a populace and take over.<br /> <br /> Consider Germany in the decades leading up to the Nazi (democratically elected) take over of the country. Despite there being latent issues with bias against Jews, Catholics, Romani, and the LGBTQ+ communities, the late 1800s and early 1900s were a time were Germany and the countries in its sphere of influence were some of the most progressive and inclusive in Europe. My great-grandfather grew up in this period outside of Munich. He felt fully integrated into Germany society. Being Jewish never hurt him as a businessman, or lessened the respect he earned from his Christian neighbors as a proud German. When WWI was starting, he considered himself a patriot, and volunteered to join the army. Being Jewish didn't stop him from quickly becoming an officer on merit. He survived and came home a war hero even with the loss, and being a Jew was never part of how people gave him respect for his service to his country. In the 20's, never in his wildest nightmares even with the Nazis around, would he think that it was possible for Germany to turn on him... until it did.<br /> <br /> Look at all the countries that the CIA fucked up. How quickly old resentments that seemed buried in the past and barely part of mainstream culture sprang to life in horrifically violent ways.<br /> <br /> To an extent, Republic City got lucky because there wasn't a follow up to the Beer Hall Putsch. It failed, and it stayed failed, and small progress towards a more equal society (though as should be noted again that fact that Bending is an inborn trait is going to mean it can never really follow real world equality) mollified the population enough that a series of major crises in a row from external threats probably helped considerably in halting the Equalists from trying to reform with new leaders. <br /> <br /> Non-Benders that didn't have significant privilege from other sources, had spent centuries in cultures with no social mobility. They weren't born into the right families, and without Bending couldn't push for more social power. Then bam, democracy is tossed into their lap. They have no experience or cultural values on how to exercise democratic power. The Equalists come in and say, &quot;we'll show you how to use your new power to claim what people like you haven't had for generations.&quot; It's not, at all, a shock to me that they'd find an audience. Humans are very poorly designed to deal with this sort of rapid change and sudden ability to massively shift around power dynamics. The show doesn't need to hit you over the head with Benders abusing non-Benders (and TLA did show anti-Bender sentiment that grew out of a mix of no Avatar, or the unilateral actions of Avatars, and the Fire Nation's war). It's self evident that people without obvious power are going to have a level of resentment towards those that lucked into power.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-03T07:47:05-07:00","id":1447195,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Konradleijon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1446592#post1446592\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That makes sense considering he was imprisoned for who knows how long</div> </div> </div> </div>For about as long as Korra\u2019s been bending, he was in there for conspiracy to kidnap her as a child which was also why she got put in a walled compound for the next dozen years.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13313-sith_happens","name":"Sith_Happens","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13313_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-03T09:56:31-07:00","id":1447222,"title":"","content":"Eh, I mean, the outcome of introducing democracy is often, that you get democracy. Like, India introduced democracy - still a flawed democracy. Democratic regression exists, but you rarely get a situation, where it is more authoritarian than the previous status quo. It was worthwhile for the United States to work towards democratic reform in Korea and Japan, the EU in Southern and Eastern Europe, ex-colonial states to work towards a post-colonial democratic status quo and so on. The &quot;Waves of Democracy&quot; generally have worked pretty well, and at least delivered more democracy than the status quo (<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waves_of_democracy\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waves_of_democracy</a>).<br /> <br /> It's true that popular democracy can be hard on small minorities with cozier relationships with aristocratic or autocratic pre-democracy elites, but, on the one hand, sometimes to make an omelette you gotta break a few eggs (e.g. benefits outweigh the harms), and on the other hand, those close relationships tend to be fragile anyway, and it's hard to argue for keeping autocracy on life-support for that reason.<br /> <br /> (Recent opinions tend to be shaped by the Iraq and Afghanistan experience, but I think it's worth trying to distance the general phenomenon from those specifics. There are objections to be levied against trying to wage large scale wars in that manner, for those ends, without succumbing to dangerous ideas that countries need to be monitored to get to a certain &quot;stage&quot; before their peoples should be &quot;allowed&quot; democracy, lest terrible perils ensue for religious/ethnic/etc minorities and democracy &quot;inevitably&quot; defeat itself. That's a dangerous way for acceptance of &quot;enlightened absolutism&quot; (or &quot;enlightened authoritarianism&quot;) to enter our heads via the back door.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-03T15:05:36-07:00","id":1447277,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ghosthead</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1447222#post1447222\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Eh, I mean, the outcome of introducing democracy is often, that you get democracy.</div> </div> </div> </div>My point really doesn't hinge on whether or not the &quot;end&quot; result is democracy, but the tendency for the rapid introduction of democracy to lead to high levels of social unrest and instability. If Iraq, like Germany before it, emerges as a true stable democracy in the next decade or so? Awesome! But that doesn't erase the disaster of transition it would be, and the dictators that had power during that transition.<br /> <br /> It's not something you're see in the Polity dataset (and even that pretty iffy metric shows that the first and second waves of democracy didn't work at all to spread democracy to the world), because it isn't designed to measure at that level of detail.<br /> <br /> You also don't see things like how much a country had proto-democratic values/ideals before a democratic government was formed vs. not. The third wave of democracy's huge expansion of democracy is very reliant on treating communism as not democratic, so all of the former Soviet states with generations of indoctrination to a form of democratic values scoring as democracies in an index biased towards liberal democracy skews the data significantly.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-05T17:59:22-07:00","id":1447674,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1447277#post1447277\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You also don't see things like how much a country had proto-democratic values/ideals before a democratic government was formed vs. not. </div> </div> </div> </div>Even in cases where there is a widely stated and culturally familiar perception of democracy such as the United States (which did exercise such things in colonial governments and inherited them from Britain), you can have cases where expansion of the franchise and other factors lead to severe corruption. Actual democratic values and ideals did not prevent the existence of Tammany Hall, the process of having men with lots of facial hair lose beard and moustache in sequence that they could vote three times.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-07T06:19:00-07:00","id":1447804,"title":"","content":"Considering only a few people could vote I don\u2019t see if it counts as \u201cdemocratic\u201d in modern sense. Also Asami\u2019s father Hiroshi Sato hates Benders cause a bender killed his wife. That\u2019s not how discrimination works.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23952-konradleijon","name":"Konradleijon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23952_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1441"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-07T09:04:00-07:00","id":1447820,"title":"","content":"I think it could be of interest if, instead of focusing solely on the unavoidable problems that changing a political system entails, one focuses also on another factor - a factor that's very core to populisms of all stripes (democratic or not. Note: Populism isn't inherently un-democratic, albeit Equalist take certainly is):<b> Identity</b><br /> <br /> A)The United Republic it's an ex-colony of the Fire Nation in Earth Nation territory, that was forced into this multicultural independant place after the war.<br /> <br /> B)Nationality in Avatar it's strongly related with bending and the rule of benders. It's in the names of the nations, but also in the way they organize their lives. Most of the &quot;technology&quot; of the nations depends on the nation's respective element (ice/water manipulation in the frozen poles, the ways walls and lifts opperate in Earth cities, even the metalurgy of the fire nation - albeit that last one it's the least dependant on magic.). The &quot;way of life&quot; in the core of the realms strongly suggests benders are better<br /> <br /> You can also see how the armies of each nation are almost entirely composed of benders of the nation's element. Only after the world get's way off balance you start seeing &quot;mixed&quot; forces. The place of nonbenders in society it's more doubtful, and with few exceptions they're most likely to be victims in any conflict between nations<br /> <br /> SO:<br /> <br /> My perception it's that Amon's rethoric was about convincing the people that the undeniable innate advantages of benders are unjust, and the world would be better if nobody had them. But this focuses more on present social issues than eugenics:<br /> <br /> Benders are called to be unfit to rule because they're responsable of the recent war - or so Amon says. He gains the sympathy of nonbenders by saying he was disfigured by a Fire Nation soldier - considering point A), I would think the peeople of Republic City had a lot of suffering in the hands of Fire Nation soldiers (and/or ex-soldiers turned bandits?)<br /> <br /> I think we could infer Equalist are people that are far too tired of being brutalized by benders. Bender criminals are just a quick, perhaps not too good, example. Hiroshi Sato's story most likely it's far from an isolated case. And now they feel they can do something about it.<br /> <br /> I would say the political changes provided most of the material conditions: A much less dominant bender caste (compared with that of the traditional Nations), a nonbender population that most likely suffered the war at hands of bender soldiers, a mixture of ethnicities weakening nationalist feelings that could legitimate the rule of benders...<br /> Republic City's &quot;multiculturalism&quot; provided the perfect place for nonbenders to reject any previous identities related to the old nations and the old order, in favor of a new identity that's also based on bending, but in a negative way (as in, not having it).<br /> <br /> Add now a new Avatar that's still learning, and thus isn't as powerful as Aang was (and that has some serious problems of attitude, that very much fall into the stereotypes Equalists are preaching: She's violent and uses her status as &quot;bender among benders&quot;, so to speak, to disrespect the law).<br /> <br /> Amon gave voice to the grievances, added the terrorist ninjas and self-defense technology to empower nonbenders into taking action, and used religion coupled with unique powers to legitimate himself as the absolute leader<br /> <br /> Of course, this is mostly speculation. I think it would be interesting if this was actually more present *in* the show. But then, time can be tyranical - the show it's short, and it's about Korra, so I think one can't really ask for many explicit details.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-07-2021, 12:56 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-07T14:43:33-07:00","id":1447848,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Konradleijon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1447804#post1447804\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Considering only a few people could vote I don\u2019t see if it counts as \u201cdemocratic\u201d in modern sense.</div> </div> </div> </div>What's the context of this comment?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also Asami\u2019s father Hiroshi Sato hates Benders cause a bender killed his wife. That\u2019s not how discrimination works. </div> </div> </div>It's not how discrimination works, but it is a psychology frequently seen in bigotry. It requires a preexisting prejudice, but esp. among people that are in the upper classes of society, there's a tendency to focus on personal trauma caused by a member of their targeted other (as if it couldn't have happened by someone in a different group) to attempt to justify the idea that it is a black and white case of victim vs. oppressor and not have to examine their own actions. Hiroshi could have chosen to use his power to push for social change that would have addressed the needs of the non-Bender community instead of choosing to act on a persecution complex.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1447820#post1447820\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think it could be of interest if, instead of focusing solely on the unavoidable problems that changing a political system entails, one focuses also on another factor - a factor that's very core to populisms of all stripes (democratic or not. Note: Populism isn't inherently un-democratic, albeit Equalist take certainly is):<b> Identity</b></div> </div> </div> </div>Agreed!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A)The United Republic it's an ex-colony of the Fire Nation in Earth Nation territory, that was forced into this multicultural independant place after the war. </div> </div> </div>I think there's a matter of how perspectives change over time. The narrative (though we only get it through a biased source) presented in the show is that in the aftermath of the 100 year war, the Republic was massively lauded by the general populace. The Fire Nation and Earth Kingdom were both amenable to ceding the city, and Aang's involvement as the Avatar who brought peace to the world gave it large scale popularity as a way to preserve that peace.<br /> <br /> It's only in the next generation(s) that the shiny glimmer of idealism starts to tarnish in the face of reality. The downsides start to seem larger as the initial fervor for the good sides diminishes, and increasing cultural exchange doesn't magically resolve lingering social resentments (both the Bender vs. non-Bender issue, and post-war trauma issues).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You can also see how the armies of each nation are almost entirely composed of benders of the nation's element. Only after the world get's way off balance you start seeing &quot;mixed&quot; forces. </div> </div> </div>I think the large point is spot on, but this one is overstated. The front-line soldiers of the Fire and Earth armies are shown as all Benders, but we see non-Benders in other military roles; esp. espionage and special operations because Bending is really flashy and doesn't really help much when stealth and subterfuge are important mission parameters. Training non-Benders in chi-blocking anti-Bending techniques isn't really something that's going to be useful for a traditional battlefield soldier. It's great for dealing with a small patrol of Benders, or assassinating a Bender general if you need to engage them in a fight.<br /> <br /> The Water Tribes are generally shown as having a more integrated military. Some of that is the Fire Nation specifically targeting Water Benders, but the Water Tribes are generally presented as having a better grasp of building a culture of mutuality between Benders and non-Benders. Though it's pretty easy to say this backs up the larger point anyway.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> My perception it's that Amon's rethoric was about convincing the people that the undeniable innate advantages of benders are unjust, and the world would be better if nobody had them. But this focuses more on present social issues than eugenics: </div> </div> </div>Yes? I mean, this feels pretty much straight from the show to me. The only reason why there's hints of eugenics in Amon's rhetoric is because Bending is a semi-inherited thing (it's not strictly genetic, but that's not really something that's ever stopped eugenicists before). You can't really talk about getting rid of Bending no matter how much you want to focus on the social inequity, without having to address the in-born talent nature of Bending.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Of course, this is mostly speculation. I think it would be interesting if this was actually more present *in* the show. But then, time can be tyranical - the show it's short, and it's about Korra, so I think one can't really ask for many explicit details. </div> </div> </div>I think a lot of this is in the show, even if not given a lot of focus. Though Kuvira voices a lot of this in her ultranationalist fascist (yes, she's definitely a fascist, but no she's not a Nazi, she's way more in-line with Franco than Hitler) populist rhetoric. After Kuvira throws a military coup of the Earth Nation with the queen assassinated, and then uses that military to bring all the local governments into her control, the Republic is a necessary target for her. It's &quot;rightfully&quot; Earth property and she's been purging non-Earth citizens from Earth territory, the Republic is protecting the heir to the throne and thus the only legitimate threat to her control of the country, she has to justify that massive military that's the source of her power with something more than roving bandits, and the Republic is the key source of alliance between the other three Nations and if it goes away the other Nations lose legitimacy to interfere with internal Earth matters.<br /> <br /> Fascist propaganda need to touch on things present in the populace. So her ability to get people behind her against the threat of the Republic means there needs to be lingering resentments towards the Republic and how it formed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-17T11:46:28-07:00","id":1448791,"title":"","content":"So the first season of Korra\u2019s allegory could be seen as how facism starts from one individual to a group that has tell know never been discriminated against. And once he\u2019s proven as a fraud facism goes away,<br /> <br /> Or how communists want to cut of out let\u2019s because it\u2019s unfair to the legless. Either way the allegory doesn\u2019t work","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23952-konradleijon","name":"Konradleijon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23952_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1441"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-17T12:10:36-07:00","id":1448793,"title":"","content":"Or the allegory is to neither of those things, and it works just fine as an allegory about how easily people can be riled up by populist rhetoric, but that populism alone isn't really a sustainable movement. And that checks out just fine.<br /> <br /> Fascism and Communism are not definition lacking terms that just mean whatever's convenient to score political points by applying labels. Amon never positions himself as either. He's not a nationalist which is an essential component of Fascism, and he's never shown as giving a shit about changing the class dynamics of society by changing how control over production is handled which is an essential component of Communism.<br /> <br /> How are we on page 4 and still beating this horse?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-18T03:34:16-07:00","id":1457091,"title":"","content":"In my opinion I think part of the issue is Legend of Korra doesnt really give the social issues it wants to explore time to breathe. I mean the show's structure really doesnt lend itself to to complex political idea's. Last airbender had about 3 seasons to go through what it wanted to go through, to compare &quot;Katara has deep underlying issues with her mothers murder&quot; has at least 1 episode and a lot of screen time in other episodes and that's a minor plot point wereas Book 1: air has to establish setting, Characters and arc in 12 episodes. It's going to struggle to really struggle with something as complex as a setting were intrinsic talents are so extreme would naturally lean towards Technocratic hierarchies and that's before we explore the meta issue that Legand of Korra relies on the projection of Western historical cultural trends onto an fantasy asian setting.<br /> The question for me isnt what themes Korra wants to explore it's if it explores them effectively enough to even be recognized which from this discussion i personally don't think it did.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26986,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","user":"Ragged Robin","date":". 08-18-2021, 06:39 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-18T16:10:12-07:00","id":1457153,"title":"","content":"I think the only flaw in the conclusion is that neiter ATLA or LoK are shows about politics. They're shows about personal conflicts set within a larger world that acknowledges how politics frames personal conflicts.<br /> <br /> The primary themes of LoK aren't the dangers of populism or the difficulty of finding a way to transition to better governmental structures. LoK wanted a more complicated political backdrop than &quot;Fire Nation imperialism needs to be stopped,&quot; so Korra's emotional struggles would be less clear cut than Aang's struggle to balance his pacifist inclinations with no way to stop the Fire Nation without violence.<br /> <br /> The political themes of LoK don't have time to breathe and get muddled because they're secondary or tertiary themes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-20T13:31:43-07:00","id":1457296,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1457153#post1457153\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think the only flaw in the conclusion is that neiter ATLA or LoK are shows about politics. They're shows about personal conflicts set within a larger world that acknowledges how politics frames personal conflicts.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think LoA and LoK are very comparable in that regards. In both cases the main conficts are arguably personal for most of the time, but I think Korra takes politics a step further from a tertiary theme to a very important secondary theme<br /> <br /> Fire Lord and his &quot;henchmen&quot; seem like the classic &quot;I'm evil 'n want to conquer/destroy the world&quot; type more than those of any real world imperialists, but that's OK because politics trully aren't important in the show and you're <i>supposed </i>to not give more than half a tought about them. Hence nobody cares. They're super filmsy if you do.<br /> <br /> Compared to that, LoK villans always proselitize about what they do, and why they do it (why it is &quot;good&quot;) - almost in every chapter. Like: Amon has a &quot;party&quot;, pampleths, tries to convince people, tries to establish a new goverment...<br /> <br /> ...compare that with AtlA glossing over how the Fire Nation does things to such an extent you even missed that they were very much colonizers (with all and the pretended ideal of &quot;civilizing&quot; the world) in spite of watching them trough 4 full seasons instead of just one, and that some of it bleeded into LoK lore. Trully, the show focuses a LOT more on the destruction, and the &quot;divine salvation&quot; that the avatar represents, than in the political structures and the actions of the people.<br /> <br /> Many of Korra's personal conflicts are in relation to the <i>political </i>role of the Avatar. From the first chapter when she believes to be above the law, to the last I saw where her symbolic place (more than her &quot;magic power level&quot;) seems to be a problem for Zaheer's Anarch agenda<br /> <br /> Compare that with Aang's never questioned role in mantaning the status quo of the 4 nations, that's always treated as a net positive (In fact, <i>isn't IT questioned in LoK</i>? The way republic city was founded seems to be a problem for Kuvira, isn't it? I don't think Aang &quot;freeing stuff&quot;, so to speak, was ever mentioned as a political problem before. Well, it was a problem for the direct antagonists but it was never explained as a political problem with a reasoning behind that could rally people towards helping the antagonists).<br /> <br /> Heck, for a &quot;pacifist&quot;, Aang seems to solve many, many, problems by figthing. But that's ok to me, after all, he's a martial artist And the show it's *about that*. Note how everything it's solved at the moment the Fire Lord and his daugther get their asses handled to them in duels <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Of course, there are &quot;explanations&quot; to this (it's not a plot hole) but it's solved rather nonchalantly, and the obvious instability this should cause it's either solved off camera, or has to wait until LoK.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The political themes of LoK don't have time to breathe and get muddled because they're secondary or tertiary themes. </div> </div> </div>I disagree. Not in that they're secondary themes, but in the rest.<br /> <br /> It would be an interestinge excercise to see how much screen time does LoK invest on each theme vs. LoA (albeit I would never be motivated enough to do it), but I do think it's political themes breathed and were muddled ... As the existance of so much content, and so complex, about this very topic (vs, say, Fire Lord's take on imperialism) seems to indicate.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 08-20-2021, 04:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-20T15:01:41-07:00","id":1457301,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1457296#post1457296\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Fire Lord and his &quot;henchmen&quot; seem like the classic &quot;I'm evil 'n want to conquer/destroy the world&quot; type more than those of any real world imperialists, but that's OK because politics trully aren't important in the show and you're <i>supposed </i>to not give more than half a tought about them. Hence nobody cares. They're super filmsy if you do.</div> </div> </div> </div>This feels pretty flimsy analytically.<br /> <br /> AtLA spends a huge amount of time on Zuko and Iroh's perspective, and gives plenty of screen time to Azula and her crew as well, all of which reveals that the Fire Nation is not meant to be some two-dimensional villain.<br /> <br /> This skips over the very nature of war as a political action. Wars only exist as a product of politics.<br /> <br /> It also skips over all the political issues of secondary/temporary antagonists in the Northern Water Tribe, and the Earth Kingdom. AtLA introduced the Earth Kingdom's secret police that brainwash you into obedience, not LoK, if we're looking for overtly political examples.<br /> <br /> Part of Aang's whole emotional journey is that he has to see the Fire Nation as more than a BBEG and nameless goons, and thus we as the audience are asked to do the same. The fact that &quot;nobody cares&quot; to me just says the more people are willing to ignore the politics of AtLA (perhaps because the show doesn't present them in a fashion that drives engagement) rather than those politics not being there. It's pretty much the central dramatic conflict of the show: Aang has to fix the imbalance of the world, but can't bring himself to do what seems like the only way to do so because that means causing extreme harm to people even if they're people on the wrong side of a conflict.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...compare that with AtlA glossing over how the Fire Nation does things to such an extent you even missed that they were very much colonizers (with all and the pretended ideal of &quot;civilizing&quot; the world) in spite of watching them trough 4 full seasons instead of just one, </div> </div> </div>Who's this &quot;you&quot; here? Lots of people noticed that just fine.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Many of Korra's personal conflicts are in relation to the <i>political </i>role of the Avatar. </div> </div> </div>Again though, so are Aang's. The role of keeping the balance between nations is an inherently political role,and the pressures of that job are what causes Aang all his emotional turmoil. He literally questions how he's supposed to do his political job without sacrificing his personal ideals, and struggles repeatedly until he finds a way to balance those drives.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It would be an interestinge excercise to see how much screen time does LoK invest on each theme vs. LoA (albeit I would never be motivated enough to do it), but I do think it's political themes breathed and were muddled ... As the existance of so much content, and so complex, about this very topic (vs, say, Fire Lord's take on imperialism) seems to indicate. </div> </div> </div>How one defines &quot;political screen time,&quot; clearly seems to be a point of contention that would require resolving as well.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-23T10:13:10-07:00","id":1457489,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1457301#post1457301\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How one defines &quot;political screen time,&quot; clearly seems to be a point of contention that would require resolving as well.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, yes. You make a claim that &quot;The political themes of LoK don't have time to breathe and get muddled ...&quot; Since this whole thread it's about that, this is a rather heavy claim. Also hard to swallow, given how much mud there is.<br /> <br /> Supposedly the reason it's that: &quot;...because they're secondary or tertiary themes. &quot;. Yet, secondary themes can be very important and can have a lot of development that would justify them getting muddled. I don't see how that's reason enough.<br /> <br /> I don't see how your premise would justify the conclusion w/o further elaboration. What would constitute &quot;having time to get muddled&quot;? They had enough time to <i>directly </i>interact with those themes at least once in nearly every chapter, it had enough time for every villan to talk about their political plataform, and to provide a lasting (if a bit tokenish) resolution that has direct relation with that plataform (the president, opening the gates of the spirit world, the whole thing with Kubira taking power from a weak king that I didn't watch) .<br /> <br /> Even leaving aside the TlA comparison, What IS having such, and why is it relevat to people having complaints (why it's wrong to complain about that)? - I think this is VERY important<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Who's this &quot;you&quot; here? Lots of people noticed that just fine. </div> </div> </div>It was about a claim that you made, but I took that claim out of context (and the context was already a bit offtopic)...Words like *particularly* or *a lot* can be interpreted in a different way within the context. Shouldn't have done that, if only to avoid muddling the waters even more.<br /> <br /> (sic), That argument, take 2:<br /> <br /> There's a lot of colony building from the fire nation, but the way that works (movement of resources and such) isn't &quot;shown on camera&quot; a whole lot. It's just a background for some episodes, while the focus it's on the Fire Nation killing people to get to the Avatar.<br /> <br /> That's how a tertiary theme works, I think: It shows what are the stakes w/o delving on them. It's background, that's not to say it's unimportant, but it's going to be naturaly glossed over. The Fire Lord minions don't need to go on about why their political agenda or their political system it's correct, because the show isn't problematizing the various political systems that are being shown.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> AtLA spends a huge amount of time on Zuko and Iroh's perspective, and gives plenty of screen time to Azula and her crew as well, all of which reveals that the Fire Nation is not meant to be some two-dimensional villain. </div> </div> </div>Having the politics of the Fire Nation as a thertiary theme isn't the same as them being &quot;two-dimensional villains&quot;. That's not my claim, my claim it's that their take on FN politics it's filmsy if you demand a lot of detail. It's the tale of a royal family (as a secondary theme to the main cast, that's also a very convinient way to solve the main issues), but the political themes themselves aren't really elaborated further:<br /> <br /> Zuko it's the right FN king because the other two are insane, there's not a single moment, however, where Zuko attempts to gain the loyalty of the fire nation's &quot;powers that be&quot; nor tries to justify his monarchy in any way. He just is the prince, duels, sits at the throne on an empty palace and declares peace. One has to &quot;infer&quot; that probably the fire nation was fed up with the former monarchs, that Fire Lord lack of a bending makes him unsuitable for the throne (\u00bfwas it ever explained on camera if that's how FN monarchy works? - I'm infering from the importance of the agni-kai), the influence that the recent defeats might have had. Etc...<br /> <br /> FN it's a monarchy and thats how it's expected to work. That doesn't make the Fire Nation, &quot;two-dimensional&quot;, though - it merely means that you need to place some pieces together to understand their dimensions. Because whatever they are it's background, the show doesn't care to problematize it.<br /> <br /> Amon, on the other hand, appears and dies talking about his agenda. All the villans do that, even the second that just wants to be satan: Why it is good, why he believes in it, why the protags are wrong. There's also a lot of &quot;show, don't tell&quot;, like with the reactions of the people and the music - that helps to paint the villans - yet the show and even the protagonists <i>also</i> argue that they &quot;have a point&quot;. That's problematizing something rigth there.<br /> ...<br /> The show doesn't go on to explain economic and geopolitical details and such minutiae because this is an action series and not a political drama - yet, <i>as far as secondary vs. terthiary themes go</i>, I would say the political agendas (that is, the organization of society) get a lot of attention, I would argue plenty more (taking in count that the show it's shorter, and has various different villans with different agendas) than what TlA gives to explaining the same things about Fire Nation imperialism.<br /> <br /> In a fictional show the characters don't just *exist*. They're made to be and talk in a certain way. I see that the rich political background of AtlA it's almost never interacted with in a direct fashion. I see how much Korra's characters like to talk about their &quot;plataforms&quot;, forcing the protags to engage with them and formulate answers ... That's why I don't quite agree about the comparison<br /> <br /> More importantly: How is that isn't &quot;enough time to breathe and get muddled&quot;?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 08-23-2021, 01:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-23T13:33:16-07:00","id":1457505,"title":"","content":"Honestly? This doesn't seem a good faith line of though to me at all. It just feels like a slew of hyperbole, nit-picks, and context removal that directly answering would actually add anything helpful to the conversation.<br /> <br /> So... what are you really getting at? Because right now it just feels like you're treating this like the defense in a criminal case and all you need to do is poke hole in the other side enough to &quot;win,&quot; rather than trying to actually interrogate an analysis of the shows.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-25T09:26:22-07:00","id":1457640,"title":"","content":"Your argument sounds a lot like &quot;Korra can't have a bad take on politics if it hasn't a take on politics&quot;.<br /> <br /> All I'm asking it's to elaborate further why you think LoK can't have muddled notions of politics if they're a secondary or terthiary theme. I don't think my comment was anything like you say, I think it was a very reasonable thing to ask for<br /> <br /> That's certainly not how I see any of this working, and that may come from a different understanding of what is a primary, secondary and thertiary theme, what are their roles in fiction. What is having time - and if not having time and still developping these themes to a secondary (or even terthiary?) isn't muddling them in and of itself.<br /> <br /> As for the rest: I exposed why I think Politics are more involved for LoK than for TlA. As a Secondary instead of a Terthiary theme (in general, for a place where politics became more prevalent to compare, I would look at the fall of Ba-Sing-Se). Albeit, now that I think about it, terthiary themes can be important, and well worth detailing, too. In particular if the author likes the &quot;show, don't tell&quot; aproach where many details can be shoved back into the background.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 08-25-2021, 12:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-25T12:07:40-07:00","id":1457643,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1446441-so-avatar-legend-of-korra-politics-came-up-once-again?p=1457640#post1457640\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Your argument sounds a lot like &quot;Korra can't have a bad take on politics if it hasn't a take on politics&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure why it does, and why any individual statement I've made that could imply that should be interpreted that way given the rest of the thread.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't think my comment was anything like you say, I think it was a very reasonable thing to ask for </div> </div> </div>How often do we really think our own posts are the bad ones?<br /> <br /> In this case however, it comes down to a single comma not being used to spark off a multipost tangent rather than a simple request for clarification around ambiguity stemming from that one non-present punctuation mark.<br /> <br /> However reasonable what you're asking might be predicated on the assumptions given doesn't really matter if those assumptions all stem from your interpretation of a sentence being justified by a typo that seems pretty easy to spot since your interpretation creates all sorts of things that are out of step with a whole thread of positions I've staked.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As for the rest: I exposed why I think Politics are more involved for LoK than for TlA. </div> </div> </div>The problem for me is that you're not really addressing the idea that AtLA has lots of involved politics, it just gets a pass because they're simplistic.<br /> <br /> AtLA basically goes: &quot;Hey look, politics! Feel bad/good about them! Now don't think about the setup because cool elemental martial arts fight!&quot;<br /> <br /> LoK basically goes: &quot;Hey look, politics! Feed bad/good about them! Korra is thinking about them too as she gets in cool elemental martial arts fights!&quot;<br /> <br /> So yes, I do think that LoK presents politics in a fashion that invites more audience engagement with thinking about the political themes present. I think the creators have basically said that was their intent. But that doesn't mean politics aren't heavily involved in AtLA, just not as narratively engaged with.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":617,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","user":"Heavy Arms","date":". 08-25-2021, 05:28 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}